	\begin{abstract}
Microwave linear analog computer (MiLAC)-aided beamforming, which performs signal processing directly in the analog domain, has recently emerged as a promising alternative to fully digital and hybrid beamforming for multi-antenna systems. However, existing studies have shown that a single lossless and reciprocal MiLAC at the transmitter cannot, in general, attain the performance of fully digital beamforming in multi-user scenarios. To overcome this limitation, we propose a two-layer MiLAC-aided transmit beamforming architecture for downlink multi-user multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) networks. Leveraging multiport microwave network theory, we first establish a general physics-compliant model of the proposed architecture, and then derive a simplified model under the assumptions of unilateral and perfectly matched amplifiers. We prove that, with lossless and reciprocal MiLACs, the two-layer MiLAC-aided transmitter can realize any fully digital transmit beamformer, while a single-layer MiLAC-aided receiver can attain the same achievable rate as a fully digital linear combiner. We then derive closed-form mappings from fully digital transmit beamformers and receive combiners to their corresponding MiLAC-aided analog implementations. Based on these equivalence results, we formulate a sum-rate maximization problem for the considered multi-user MIMO network and develop an optimization framework for jointly designing the power allocation and scattering matrices of the MiLAC-aided transceivers. Numerical results validate the theoretical analysis and show that the proposed two-layer MiLAC-aided design attains the same sum-rate performance as its fully digital counterpart.
	\end{abstract}

	\begin{IEEEkeywords}
		Microwave linear analog computer (MiLAC), multi-user multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO), sum-rate maximization.
	\end{IEEEkeywords}

	\section{Introduction}
	The rapid growth of wireless traffic, connected devices, and data-intensive applications is imposing increasingly strict requirements on future wireless networks in terms of data rate, spectral efficiency, coverage, and connectivity. To meet these demands, multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems are evolving beyond the massive MIMO architectures deployed in fifth-generation (5G) networks toward ultra-massive, or \emph{gigantic}, MIMO systems comprising hundreds or even thousands of antennas. Such large-scale antenna arrays can provide substantial beamforming and spatial-multiplexing gains, making them particularly attractive for future wireless systems operating at higher carrier frequencies, where large array gains are essential to compensate for the more severe propagation losses \cite{giganticMIMO, OnTheRoad}.


The scalability of conventional MIMO transceiver architectures, however, becomes increasingly challenging as the number of antennas grows. In a \textit{fully digital beamforming} architecture, each antenna is connected to a dedicated radio-frequency (RF) chain comprising data converters, mixers, and other RF components. Although this architecture provides full beamforming flexibility by allowing the magnitude and phase of every beamforming coefficient to be independently controlled, its hardware cost and power consumption scale with the number of antennas. In addition, precoding and combining require digital matrix-vector operations on a per-symbol basis, resulting in a considerable computational burden for gigantic antenna arrays. \textit{Hybrid digital-analog beamforming} was introduced as a more scalable alternative, in which a low-dimensional digital beamformer is combined with an analog beamforming network, typically implemented using phase shifters \cite{ps-hbf,yu2016alternating}. By reducing the number of RF chains, hybrid beamforming substantially alleviates the hardware cost of fully digital architectures. Nevertheless, phase-shifter (PS)-based analog beamforming is subject to constant-modulus constraints and therefore offers less beamforming flexibility than fully digital beamforming. Moreover, since part of the signal processing remains in the digital domain, hybrid architectures still require digital precoding and combining, together with digital-to-analog and analog-to-digital conversion in the RF chains.


More recently, \textit{microwave linear analog computers (MiLACs)} have emerged as a fundamentally different approach to large-scale MIMO beamforming \cite{nerini2025milac1,nerini2025milac2,milac-mag}. A MiLAC is a reconfigurable multiport microwave network composed of tunable impedance elements to directly perform configurable linear transformations on RF signals as they propagate
through the network. At the transmitter, the data streams are supplied to the input ports of the MiLAC through RF chains, while its output ports are connected to the transmit antennas. At the receiver, a MiLAC can directly combine the signals collected by the antenna array before analog-to-digital conversion. Thus, precoding and combining are performed entirely in the analog domain rather than through symbol-by-symbol digital computations. This architecture offers several appealing features for gigantic MIMO. In particular, the number of RF chains can scale with the number of data streams rather than the number of antennas. Moreover, the RF chains only need to generate and detect constellation symbols, rather than precisely reproduce antenna-wise beamformed signals, allowing lower-resolution digital-to-analog converters (DACs) and analog-to-digital converters (ADCs). Computationally demanding beamforming operations can also be performed directly by the microwave network, eliminating the need for digital matrix-vector multiplication at every symbol interval~\cite{nerini2025milac2}.	
The potential of MiLAC-aided communications has stimulated growing research interest. For point-to-point MIMO systems, a single layer of lossless and reciprocal MiLAC at both the transmitter and receiver was shown to remain capacity-achieving, i.e., to attain the same channel capacity as a fully digital transceiver \cite{nerini2025milac-capacity}. Several subsequent studies have further investigated the architectures and applications of MiLAC. In particular, the fully connected MiLAC architecture was shown to require a number of tunable impedance components that grows quadratically with the number of ports. This motivates the development of stem-connected MiLAC architectures \cite{nerini2025mimo}, inspired by stem-connected beyond diagonal reconfigurable intelligent
surface (BD-RIS) architectures \cite{qstem,wu2025band}. By exploiting a graph-theoretical representation of the microwave network, stem-connected MiLACs reduce the circuit-complexity from quadratic to linear scaling with the number of antennas while preserving the capacity-achieving property in point-to-point MIMO systems. 
Recent studies have addressed a range of practical considerations in MiLAC design, including channel estimation \cite{milac-ce, milac-ce-2026}, losses \cite{lossy-milac}, discrete elements \cite{stem-zhang,finite-milac}, mutual coupling \cite{physics-milac}, wideband effects \cite{wideband-milac,wideband-milac-journal}, energy efficiency \cite{milac-EE,ee-milac-all}, and impedance mismatch \cite{general-milac}.
MiLAC has also been studied in a broader range of applications, including multi-user communications \cite{multiuser-milac, limits-milac}, MIMO radar sensing and direction-of-arrival (DoA) estimation \cite{sensing-milac}, and joint active and passive beamforming \cite{passive-active-milac}. These advances underscore the potential of MiLAC as a versatile analog-computing platform for future large-scale wireless transceivers.

Despite this progress, an important limitation arises when one-layer MiLAC-aided beamforming is extended from point-to-point MIMO to multi-user communications. For a lossless and reciprocal MiLAC, the set of physically realizable beamforming matrices is smaller than that available to a fully digital architecture. Consequently, although a single-layer MiLAC is capacity-achieving in point-to-point MIMO systems, it cannot in general achieve the same beamforming flexibility as a fully digital transmitter in multi-user multiple-input single-output (MISO) systems \cite{multiuser-milac, limits-milac}. This limitation becomes particularly pronounced when the beamforming vectors required for different users are strongly correlated \cite{limits-milac}. While the performance gap may decrease in gigantic MIMO regimes as user channels become increasingly orthogonal, it remains severe in finite-dimensional multi-user systems.
One approach to overcoming this limitation is the \textit{hybrid digital-MiLAC} architecture \cite{limits-milac}. By introducing a low-dimensional digital beamformer before the MiLAC, this architecture achieves the same beamforming flexibility as a fully digital transmitter while using only as many RF chains as data streams. However, introducing such digital precoding sacrifices one of the key advantages of MiLAC-aided beamforming. The signals entering the RF chains are no longer simply the original constellation symbols but linear combinations generated by digital precoding. Consequently, higher-resolution DACs are
needed to accurately represent these signals, and per-symbol digital operations are reintroduced. 
Whether MiLAC architectures can achieve the full flexibility and performance of digital beamforming in general multi-user systems while remaining fully analog and using low-resolution data converters remains an open question. In this work, we propose a novel \textit{two-layer MiLAC} architecture comprising two lossless and reciprocal MiLAC networks interconnected through a power-allocation stage. The key finding is that this additional analog-processing layer overcomes the fundamental limitation of the one-layer architecture. For any fully digital precoding matrix, an equivalent two-layer MiLAC configuration can be constructed, thereby reproducing the same transmit beamforming operation without any digital precoder. It offers the beamforming flexibility of fully digital transmission while retaining key advantages of MiLAC, including a number of RF chains that scales with the number of transmitted streams, the potential use of low-resolution DACs, and fully analog symbol-level beamforming.
 The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}
	\item We propose a two-layer MiLAC architecture in which two reconfigurable microwave networks are interconnected through an active power-allocation stage. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first MiLAC architecture capable of realizing any fully digital precoding matrix in a multi-user MIMO system while performing beamforming entirely in the analog domain.
	\item We establish a physics-compliant model for the proposed two-layer architecture based on multiport microwave network theory, explicitly accounting for the interactions among the two MiLAC networks, the intermediate amplifiers, and their interconnections. Under the assumptions of unilateral and perfectly matched amplifiers, we further simplify this model to obtain a more tractable formulation.
	\item We analytically characterize the beamforming capabilities of the proposed two-layer MiLAC-aided transmitter and one-layer MiLAC-aided receiver. The different layer requirements arise because scaling the receive combiner does not affect the achievable rate, whereas scaling the transmit beamformer reduces the radiated power and may therefore lead to performance degradation under a fixed transmit-power budget. For any feasible fully digital precoding matrix, we show that there exists a two-layer MiLAC configuration that realizes exactly the same linear transformation. At the receiver, we show that a suitably scaled and conjugated fully digital minimum mean-square error (MMSE) combiner can be exactly implemented by a single-layer MiLAC without any loss in achievable rate. Closed-form constructions of the corresponding MiLAC scattering matrices are further derived. 
	\item We provide numerical results to validate the developed model and the theoretical analysis. The proposed two-layer architecture is compared with fully digital beamforming, one-layer MiLAC, hybrid digital-MiLAC, and PS-based hybrid beamforming under representative multi-user MIMO configurations. The results demonstrate that the proposed transceiver attains the same sum-rate performance as its fully digital counterpart while retaining the analog-processing advantages of MiLAC.
\end{itemize}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:proposed-architecture} introduces the physics-compliant and simplified model for the proposed two-layer MiLAC architecture. Section~\ref{Sec:system model} introduces the multi-user MIMO system model for a MiLAC-aided network. Section~\ref{sec:optimality} investigates the optimality of the proposed transceiver architecture. Section~\ref{sec:pro-alg} develops the corresponding problem formulation and algorithm design. Section~\ref{sec:sim} presents numerical results, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.

\section{Proposed two-layer MiLAC architecture}\label{sec:proposed-architecture}
	In this section, we propose a two-layer MiLAC architecture comprising two MiLACs interconnected through a set of $D$ amplifiers. Specifically, we first develop a general physics-compliant model of the proposed architecture based on multiport microwave network theory. We then derive a simplified model under the assumptions of unilateral and perfectly matched amplifiers.

	\begin{figure}[!t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{figure/2layer_model_S.eps}
		\caption{Block diagram of two-layer MiLAC-aided network. }
		\label{Fig:sys-model-S}
 \vspace{-0.5cm}
	\end{figure} 

As depicted in Fig.~\ref{Fig:sys-model-S}, the proposed two-layer MiLAC-aided architecture receives $D$ input signals from $D$ RF chains and provides $L$ output signals to the transmit antennas. It comprises two cascaded MiLAC networks interconnected by $D$ parallel amplifiers. MiLAC 1 has $M_1=2D$ ports, comprising $D$ input ports connected to the RF chains and $D$ output ports connected to the amplifiers. MiLAC 2 has $M_2=D+L$ ports, comprising $D$ input ports connected to the amplifiers and $L$ output ports connected to the transmit antennas. The input vector $\mathbf{s}=[s_1,\ldots,s_D]^{\mathsf T}$ is first processed by MiLAC~1 in the analog domain. The resulting $D$ signals undergo amplitude adjustment and then are fed into MiLAC~2 for further analog processing. The resulting output vector is $\mathbf{x}=[x_1,\ldots,x_L]^{\mathsf T}$.

\subsection{MiLAC Modeling}\label{sec:MiLAC modeling}
According to microwave network theory, a multiport MiLAC can be characterized by a tunable admittance matrix whose entries depend on the network's adjustable admittance components. This representation applies to both the existing one-layer architecture and each MiLAC in the proposed two-layer architecture. Focusing on the latter, we denote the admittance matrix of the $m$-th MiLAC by $\mathbf{Y}_m\in\mathbb{C}^{M_m\times M_m}$, where $m\in\{1,2\}$ and $M_m$ denotes its number of ports.
Since the MiLACs considered in this work are fully connected, each port-$v$ of MiLAC $m$ is connected to ground through an admittance $Y_{m,v,v}\in\mathbb{C}$ and is interconnected with port-$i$ through an admittance $Y_{m,i,v}\in\mathbb{C}$ for all $i\neq v$. Let $\mathcal{V}_m = \{1,\ldots,M_m\}$ denote the port index set of MiLAC~$m$. As a function of these tunable admittance components, the $(i,v)$-th entry of $\mathbf{Y}_m$, for $i,v\in\mathcal{V}_m$, is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:admittance-matrix}
	[\mathbf{Y}_m]_{i,v}
	=
	\begin{cases}
		-Y_{m,i,v}, & i\neq v,[1mm]
		\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{M_m}Y_{m,j,v}, & i=v,
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
which follows from Ohm's law \cite{nerini2025milac1}.
\begin{figure}[t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{figure/milac.eps}
	\caption{Example of lossless and reciprocal MiLAC with $ 6 $ ports. }
	\label{Fig:milac}
	\vspace{-0.6cm}
\end{figure}  
In the proposed two-layer MiLAC architecture, both MiLACs are assumed to be lossless and reciprocal. As an illustrative example, Fig.~\ref{Fig:milac} shows a fully connected lossless and reciprocal MiLAC with $2$ input ports and $4$ output ports. In the diagram, the blue admittances represent inter-port connections, while the orange admittances indicate ports connected to ground.
Under the lossless assumption, all tunable admittance components of both MiLACs are purely imaginary and can be written as $	Y_{m,i,v}=jB_{m,i,v}$, where $B_{m,i,v}\in\mathbb{R}$. Consequently, the admittance matrix of MiLAC $m$ can be expressed as $\mathbf{Y}_m=j\mathbf{B}_m$, where $\mathbf{B}_m=\mathbf{B}_m^{\mathsf T}$ and $
\mathbf{B}_m\in\mathbb{R}^{M_m\times M_m}$ is the corresponding susceptance matrix.
Let $\bm{\Theta}_m\in\mathbb{C}^{M_m\times M_m}$ denote the scattering matrix associated with MiLAC $m$. According to the relationship between the admittance and scattering matrices
\cite{nerini2025milac-capacity,nerini2025mimo,limits-milac}, we have
\begin{equation}
 \bm{\Theta}_m
 =
 \left(
 \mathbf I_{M_m}
 -j\frac{\mathbf B_m}{Y_0}
 \right)
 \left(
 \mathbf I_{M_m}
 +j\frac{\mathbf B_m}{Y_0}
 \right)^{-1}.
 \label{eq:cayley}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the susceptance matrix $\mathbf B_m$ induces a
symmetric and unitary scattering matrix $\bm{\Theta}_m$. Accordingly, it satisfies
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:unified-MiLAC-constraints}
	\begin{align}
		\bm{\Theta}_m^{\mathsf H}\bm{\Theta}_m
		&=\mathbf{I}_{M_m},
		\label{eq:unitary-constraint}
		\bm{\Theta}_m
		&=\bm{\Theta}_m^{\mathsf T},
		\label{eq:symmetric-constraint}
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
for all $m\in\{1,2\}$. Hence, MiLAC~1 and MiLAC~2 share the same mathematical representation and differ
only in their numbers of ports $M_m$.
\subsection{Amplifier Modeling}
Let $\mathcal{D}=\{1,\ldots,D\}$ denote the amplifier index set. The $d$-th amplifier is modeled as a linear two-port network \cite[Chapter 12.1]{pozar2021microwave} with scattering matrix
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{S}_d
	=
	\begin{bmatrix}
		S_{d,11} & S_{d,12}
		S_{d,21} & S_{d,22}
	\end{bmatrix}
	\in\mathbb{C}^{2\times2},
	\qquad d\in\mathcal{D},
\end{equation}
where all scattering parameters are defined with respect to a common real reference impedance $Z_0$. Let $a_{d,1}$ and $a_{d,2}$ denote the incident waves at ports~1 and~2 of the $d$-th amplifier, respectively, while $b_{d,1}$ and $b_{d,2}$ denote the corresponding outgoing waves. Their scattering relation is given by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{bmatrix}
		b_{d,1}
		b_{d,2}
	\end{bmatrix}
	=
	\mathbf{S}_d
	\begin{bmatrix}
		a_{d,1}
		a_{d,2}
	\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Here, $S_{d,11}$ and $S_{d,22}$ are the input and output reflection coefficients, respectively, while $S_{d,21}$ and $S_{d,12}$ denote the forward and reverse transmission coefficients, respectively.
To model the $D$ parallel amplifiers as a single network, we collect the incident and outgoing waves at each port across all branches as
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
		\mathbf{a}_m
	=&
	\begin{bmatrix}
		a_{1,m}&\cdots&a_{D,m}
	\end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf T},
	\mathbf{b}_m
	=&
	\begin{bmatrix}
		b_{1,m}&\cdots&b_{D,m}
	\end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf T},
	m\in\{1,2\}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The amplifier bank is therefore characterized by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{bmatrix}
		\mathbf{b}_1
		\mathbf{b}_2
	\end{bmatrix}
	=
	\underbrace{
		\begin{bmatrix}
 \mathbf{S}_{A,11} & \mathbf{S}_{A,12}
 \mathbf{S}_{A,21} & \mathbf{S}_{A,22}
	\end{bmatrix}}_{\mathbf{S}_A}
	\begin{bmatrix}
		\mathbf{a}_1
		\mathbf{a}_2
	\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{S}_A\in\mathbb{C}^{2D\times2D}$. Since the amplifier branches operate independently, the four submatrices are diagonal and given by
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{S}_{A,mn}
	=
	\operatorname{diag}
	\left(S_{1,mn},\ldots,S_{D,mn}\right),
	\quad m,n\in\{1,2\}.
\end{equation}
\subsection{End-to-End Modeling of the Two-Layer MiLAC Network}
The proposed two-layer MiLAC architecture can be viewed as a cascade of three multiport networks: MiLAC~1, $D$ amplifiers, and MiLAC~2. According to the MiLAC modeling in Section \ref{sec:MiLAC modeling}, we partition the scattering matrices of MiLAC $m$ as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Theta-partition}
	\bm\Theta_m=
	\begin{bmatrix}
		\bm\Theta_{m,11} & \bm\Theta_{m,21}^\mathsf{T}
		\bm\Theta_{m,21} & \bm\Theta_{m,22}
	\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $\bm\Theta_{m,11}\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times D}$, $\bm\Theta_{m,21}\in\mathbb{C}^{(M_m-D)\times D}$, and $\bm\Theta_{m,22}\in\mathbb{C}^{(M_m-D)\times (M_m-D)}$. To characterize the cascade of these multiport networks while accounting for the interactions among their interconnected ports, we first introduce the following proposition.
