	Table~\ref{tab:transceiver} compares the hardware requirements and sum-rate capabilities of different transceiver architectures. In the conventional fully digital architecture, each	transmit or receive antenna is connected to a dedicated RF chain, resulting in $L$ RF chains at the transmitter and $N_k$ RF chains at user $k$, together with high-resolution DACs and ADCs. In contrast, the MiLAC-based architectures reduce the number of RF chains to the number of transmitted and received data streams, i.e., $D$ at the transmitter and $D_k$ at user $k$. The one-layer MiLAC transmitter and receiver architecture further enables	the use of low-resolution data converters, but its constrained analog beamforming capability prevents it from attaining the sum-rate performance of a fully digital transceiver. The hybrid digital-MiLAC architecture at the transmitter can recover the fully digital beamforming performance, but requires digital precoding and correspondingly higher-resolution data converters. Moreover, with the same number of high-resolution RF chains, digital-PS hybrid architecture still cannot recover the fully digital beamforming performance. By comparison, the proposed transceiver, consisting of a two-layer MiLAC transmitter and a	one-layer MiLAC receiver, simultaneously reduces the number of RF	chains and retains the potential to employ low-resolution data converters. As established in the next section, it can also attain the same maximum sum-rate as the fully digital transceiver under the considered system model. Therefore, the proposed architecture combines the beamforming flexibility of fully digital processing with the reduced number of RF chains and analog-processing advantages of MiLAC.



\section{Fully Digital Performance Equivalence of the Proposed Transceiver}\label{sec:optimality}
In this section, we rigorously prove that the proposed MiLAC-aided transceiver achieves fully digital performance under the considered system model. Specifically, we first show that the two-layer MiLAC architecture at the transmitter can exactly realize an arbitrary fully digital transmit beamformer.
We then demonstrate that a single-layer MiLAC architecture at each receiver can attain the same achievable rate as optimal fully digital linear receive combining.
\subsection{Fully Digital Equivalence at the Transmitter}\label{sec:optimality-tx}
In this subsection, we establish that the proposed two-layer MiLAC architecture at the transmitter can exactly realize an arbitrary fully digital transmit beamformer under the considered ideal MiLAC model. The main result is stated in Theorem~\ref{the:MiLAC-HBF}.
Before introducing the theorem, we first review the relationship of the data symbol vector and the transmit signal in a fully digital system \cite{nerini2025milac-capacity}:
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{x}=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{P}_d\mathbf{s},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{P}_d\in \mathbb{C}^{L \times D}$ is the digital beamforming matrix.
\begin{theorem}\label{the:MiLAC-HBF}
	Consider a fully digital beamformer $\mathbf{P}_d \in \mathbb{C}^{L \times D}$ for the considered multi-user MIMO system.  Let its compact singular value decomposition (SVD) be
	\begin{align}\label{eq:Pd-SVD}
		\mathbf{P}_d
 =
 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1 \;
 \overline{\bm{\Sigma}}\;
 \overline{\mathbf{V}}^{\mathsf H},
	\end{align}
	where
$\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1\in\mathbb{C}^{L\times D}$ has orthonormal
columns, $\overline{\bm{\Sigma}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}$ is diagonal and
contains the singular values of $\mathbf{P}_d$, and
$\overline{\mathbf{V}}\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times D}$ is unitary. Let
$\overline{\mathbf{U}}
=
[\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1,\overline{\mathbf{U}}_2]
\in\mathbb{C}^{L\times L}$
be a unitary completion of $\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1$.
Then, under the ideal two-layer MiLAC model considered in Section~II, there always exists a feasible configuration of the two MiLAC layers and the intermediate $D$ amplifiers whose effective beamforming
matrix is exactly $\mathbf{P}_d$. One such construction is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:theta-star}
 \bm{\Theta}_1^\star
 =
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \mathbf{0}_{D\times D}
 &
 \overline{\mathbf{V}}^{\mathsf *}

 \overline{\mathbf{V}}^{\mathsf H}
 &
 \mathbf{0}_{D\times D}
 \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:phi-star}
 \bm{\Theta}_2^\star
 =
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \mathbf{0}_{D\times D}
 &
 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^{\mathsf T}

 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1
 &
 -\overline{\mathbf{U}}_2
 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_2^{\mathsf T}
 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
The corresponding scattering matrix for the amplifiers $\mathbf S_{A,21}^\star \in \mathbb{R}^{D\times D}$ and power-allocation matrix $\mathbf{P}^\star \in \mathbb{R}^{D\times D}$ are
given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:g-star}
 \mathbf{S}_{A,21}^\star
 =
 \overline{\bm{\Sigma}},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:p-star}
 (\mathbf{P}^\star)^{\frac{1}{2}}
 =
 \mathbf{S}_{A,21}^\star
 =
 \overline{\bm{\Sigma}}.
\end{equation}
Accordingly, the first- and second-stage analog beamforming matrices $\mathbf{F}^\star \in \mathbb{C}^{D \times D}, \mathbf{W}^\star \in \mathbb{C}^{L \times D}$ are
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{F}^\star
 =
 [\bm{\Theta}_1^\star]_{D+1:2D,\,1:D}
 =
 \overline{\mathbf{V}}^{\mathsf H},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{W}^\star
 =
 [\bm{\Theta}_2^\star]_{D+1:L+D,\,1:D}
 =
 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1.
\end{equation}
The resulting effective transmit beamformer satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{W}^\star
 (\mathbf{P}^\star)^{\frac{1}{2}}
 \mathbf{F}^\star
 =
 \mathbf{P}_d.
\end{equation}
Hence, the proposed two-layer MiLAC architecture can exactly reproduce any fully digital transmit beamformer under the considered ideal model.
\end{theorem}
\textit{Proof:}
The construction follows directly from the SVD in \eqref{eq:Pd-SVD}. Specifically, MiLAC~1 realizes the right singular directions $\overline{\mathbf{V}}^{\mathsf H}$, while MiLAC~2 realizes the left
singular directions $\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1$.
The intermediate $D$ amplifiers implement the corresponding singular
values through $\mathbf{S}_{A,21}^\star=\overline{\bm{\Sigma}}$.
The scattering matrices in \eqref{eq:theta-star} and \eqref{eq:phi-star} are symmetric and unitary, and are therefore
feasible under the adopted MiLAC scattering-matrix model. Consequently, $\mathbf{W}^\star
(\mathbf{P}^\star)^{\frac{1}{2}}
\mathbf{F}^\star
=
\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1
\overline{\bm{\Sigma}}\;
\overline{\mathbf{V}}^{\mathsf H}
=
\mathbf{P}_d$.
The detailed feasibility verification is provided in
Appendix~\ref{app:proof-theorem1}.
\hfill$\square$
Theorem~\ref{the:MiLAC-HBF} shows that the additional MiLAC layer
removes the beamforming restriction imposed by a
one-layer MiLAC transmitter. The result also provides a direct interpretation of the proposed architecture: the two MiLAC layers realize the left and right singular directions of the fully digital beamformer, while the intermediate amplifiers implement its singular values. Hence, under the considered ideal model, the proposed transmitter recovers the full beamforming flexibility of a fully digital architecture without introducing digital precoding.
\subsection{Fully Digital Equivalence at the Receiver}\label{sec:optimality-rx}
In this subsection, we show that a single-layer MiLAC architecture at each receiver can attain the same achievable rate as optimal fully digital linear receive combining. The main result is stated in Theorem~\ref{the:opt-1MiLAC}.
\begin{theorem}\label{the:opt-1MiLAC}
	Consider the downlink multi-user MIMO system
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:re-signal}
		\mathbf y_k
		= \frac{1}{2}
		\mathbf H_k
		\sum_{i=1}^{K}\mathbf G_i\mathbf s_i
		+
		\mathbf n_k,
		\qquad k\in\mathcal K,
	\end{equation}
	where 
	$\mathbf G_i\in\mathbb C^{L\times D_i}$ is the transmit precoder for user $i$, $\mathbf{H}_k\in\mathbb{C}^{N_k\times L}$ denotes the channel matrix from the transmitter to user~$k$ and
	$\mathbf n_k\sim
	\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf 0,\sigma_k^2\mathbf I_{N_k})$, with
	$\sigma_k^2>0$. Assume that
	$\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k)=D_k$ for all
	$k\in\mathcal K$, and that multi-user interference is treated as noise.
 For user~$k$, define the set of receive combining matrices $\{\mathbf U_k\in\mathbb C^{N_k\times D_k}\}_{k=1}^K$ realizable by a fully connected, lossless, and reciprocal one-layer MiLAC as
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 \mathcal U_k^{\mathrm{MiLAC}}
 =
 \big\{\mathbf U_k=
 \frac{1}{2}
 [\bm{\Psi}_k]_{D_k+1:D_k+N_k,\,1:D_k}
 \; | \;
 \bm{\Psi}_k^{\mathsf H}\bm{\Psi}_k
 =
 \mathbf I_{N_k+D_k},
 \;
 \bm{\Psi}_k
 =
 \bm{\Psi}_k^{\mathsf T}
 \big\}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
	Then, for any fixed set of transmit precoders $\{\mathbf G_i\}_{i=1}^{K}$, a single MiLAC layer at each receiver achieves the optimal rate attainable with unconstrained fully digital linear receive combining, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
 \max_{\mathbf U_k\in\mathcal U_k^{\mathrm{MiLAC}}}
 R_k
 =
 \max_{\mathbf U_k\in\mathbb C^{N_k\times D_k}}
 R_k,
 \qquad
 \forall k\in\mathcal K.
\end{equation}
Hence, under the stated assumptions, a single-layer MiLAC at each receiver is sufficient to attain the optimal fully digital linear-combining rate.
\end{theorem}
\textit{Proof:}
For a fixed set of transmit precoders, the optimal fully digital linear combining performance for user~$k$ can be attained by the MMSE combiner $\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}\in\mathbb C^{N_k\times D_k}$. A single-layer MiLAC can realize any combining matrix whose spectral norm does not exceed $1/2$ \cite{limits-milac}. Although $\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}$ may not directly satisfy this constraint, it can be scaled without affecting the achievable rate.
Specifically, define $\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k
 =
 c_k\left(\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}\right)^*$ and $c_k
 =
 \min\{
 1,\,
 \frac{1}{2\|\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}\|_2}
 \}$, such that $\|\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k\|_2\leq 1/2$. Since $c_k\neq 0$, this uniform scaling affects the desired signal, interference, and noise components equally, leaving the achievable rate unchanged.
Hence, $\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k$ satisfies the MiLAC spectral-norm constraint while achieving the same rate as $\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}$.
Using an SVD-based construction, we obtain a symmetric and unitary scattering matrix $\bm{\Psi}_k$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1}{2}
 [\bm{\Psi}_k]_{D_k+1:D_k+N_k,\,1:D_k}
 =
 \widetilde{\mathbf U}_k.
\end{equation}
Therefore, a single MiLAC layer can realize $\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k$ and achieve the same rate as the optimal fully digital linear receive combiner. The detailed construction and proof are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:proof-theorem2}.
\hfill$\square$
Theorem~\ref{the:opt-1MiLAC} highlights a fundamental difference between transmit and receive beamforming with a single-layer MiLAC. A single-layer MiLAC can realize the beamforming directions of an arbitrary linear beamformer after an appropriate scaling to satisfy its spectral-norm constraint. At the transmitter, such a scaling directly reduces the radiated signal power and therefore generally incurs a performance loss for a given transmit power constraint. At the receiver, in contrast, a nonzero scaling of the combining matrix scales the desired signal, interference, and noise by the same factor and hence leaves the achievable rate unchanged. Consequently, one layer of MiLAC is sufficient for rate-optimal receive combining, whereas an additional MiLAC layer is required at the transmitter to reproduce a fully digital beamformer without incurring this power loss.
\begin{remark}
	 A symmetric-unitary scattering matrix $\bm\Theta$ admits a finite-susceptance realization through the inverse Cayley transform if and only if	
	$
	\det(\mathbf I+\bm\Theta)\neq 0,
	$
	or equivalently, $-1\notin\operatorname{spec}(\bm\Theta)$. Thus, symmetry and unitarity alone do not guarantee finite-susceptance realizability. This additional condition can be satisfied without affecting the equivalence results. 	For the receive architecture in Theorem \ref{the:opt-1MiLAC}, consider the phase rotation
	$
	\widetilde{\bm\Psi}_k=e^{j\phi_k}\bm\Psi_k.
	$ This transformation preserves symmetry and unitarity. Since $\bm\Psi_k$ has finitely many eigenvalues, $\phi_k$ can always be chosen such that $-1\notin\operatorname{spec}(\widetilde{\bm\Psi}_k)$. The resulting combiner $\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k=e^{j\phi_k}\mathbf U_k$ achieves the same rate as $\mathbf U_k$. For the transmit architecture in Theorem \ref{the:MiLAC-HBF}, the phase freedom in the SVD-based construction can likewise be exploited to exclude $-1$ from the spectra of both scattering matrices while preserving the realized transmit beamformer.
\end{remark}
\section{Sum-Rate Optimization and Transceiver Design}\label{sec:pro-alg}
In this section, we first formulate the sum-rate maximization problem for the proposed MiLAC-aided transceiver based on the system model in Section~\ref{Sec:system model}. We then exploit the fully digital equivalence results established in Section~\ref{sec:optimality} to transform the MiLAC-aided transceiver design into an equivalent fully digital optimization problem, followed by a closed-form MiLAC realization.
\subsection{MiLAC-Aided Sum-Rate Maximization}
To characterize the maximum achievable sum rate of the proposed MiLAC-aided transceiver, we jointly optimize the transmitter- and receiver-side MiLAC configurations together with the intermediate power allocation. Specifically, we formulate the following sum-rate maximization problem subject to the power-allocation constraint and the structural constraints imposed by the MiLAC networks:
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:pro-formula}
 \begin{align}
 \max_{\bm{\Theta}_1,\,\bm{\Theta}_2,\,\mathbf{P},
 \{\bm{\Psi}_k\}_{k=1}^{K}}
 \quad
 & \sum_{k=1}^{K}
 R_k
 \left(
 \bm{\Theta}_1,\bm{\Theta}_2,\mathbf{P},
 \{\bm{\Psi}_i\}_{i=1}^{K}
 \right)

 \operatorname{s.t.}\quad

 & \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{P})\leq P_t
 \label{eq:con-power},
 & \eqref{eq:scattering-constraints}.
 \notag
 \end{align}
\end{subequations}
Constraints~\eqref{eq:scattering-constraints} enforce the
symmetry and unitarity of the scattering matrices, thereby ensuring
consistency with the adopted lossless and reciprocal MiLAC model.
Constraint~\eqref{eq:con-power} imposes the power-allocation budget on the intermediate amplifier stage. 
Problem~\eqref{eq:pro-formula} is highly nonconvex because the optimization variables are coupled through the achievable-rate expressions, while the scattering matrices are subject to nonconvex symmetry and unitarity constraints.
Rather than directly solving \eqref{eq:pro-formula}, we exploit the equivalence theorems established in Section~\ref{sec:optimality} to derive a fully digital formulation, as described in the next subsection.








\subsection{Equivalent Fully Digital Optimization and MiLAC Realization}
Building on the equivalence theorems established in Section~\ref{sec:optimality}, we propose the following two-step approach:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Step 1:} Solve the sum-rate maximization problem for a conventional fully digital transceiver to obtain the corresponding transmit beamforming matrix $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{d}}^\star\in\mathbb{C}^{L\times D}$ and the MMSE receive combining matrices $\{\mathbf{U}_{k}^{\mathrm{d},\star}\}_{k=1}^{K}$.

	\item \textbf{Step 2:} Given $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{d}}^\star$, apply Theorem~\ref{the:MiLAC-HBF} to construct the transmitter-side scattering matrices
 $\bm{\Theta}_1^\star$ and $\bm{\Theta}_2^\star$, together with the intermediate power-allocation matrix $\mathbf{P}^\star$. Similarly, given $\{\mathbf{U}_{k}^{\mathrm d,\star}\}_{k=1}^{K}$, apply Theorem~\ref{the:opt-1MiLAC} to construct a feasible receiver-side scattering matrix $\bm{\Psi}_k^\star$ for each user.
\end{itemize}
Since the closed-form MiLAC realizations in Step~2 are provided by Theorems~\ref{the:MiLAC-HBF} and~\ref{the:opt-1MiLAC}, the remaining task is to determine the fully digital transmit beamformer and the corresponding receive combiners in Step~1.


	For a conventional multi-user MIMO system, let $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm d}
 =
 [\mathbf{P}_1,\mathbf{P}_2,\ldots,\mathbf{P}_K]
 \in\mathbb{C}^{L\times D}$, where
$\mathbf{P}_k\in\mathbb{C}^{L\times D_k}$
denotes the transmit beamformer for user~$k$.
The fully digital sum-rate maximization problem is formulated as 
	\begin{subequations}\label{eq:pro-formula-di}
		\begin{align}
 \max_{\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm d},
 \{\mathbf{U}_k^{\mathrm d}\}_{k=1}^{K}}
 \quad
 & \sum_{k=1}^{K}R_k^{\mathrm d}

 \operatorname{s.t.}\quad
 & \sum_{k=1}^{K} \operatorname{tr} \left( \mathbf{P}_k\mathbf{P}_k^{\mathsf H}
 \right)
 \leq P_t,
 \label{eq:power-digital}
		\end{align}
	\end{subequations}
	where 
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:rate-di}
		R_k^d = \log_2 \det \left( \mathbf{I} + 	{\mathbf{U}_k^d}^\mathsf{H}  \mathbf{H}_k\mathbf{P}_k\mathbf{P}_k^\mathsf{H}\mathbf{H}_k^\mathsf{H}\mathbf{U}_k^d\tilde{\mathbf{J}}_k^{-1} \right),
	\end{equation}
	and $\tilde{\mathbf{J}}_k=\sum_{i \neq k}{\mathbf{U}_k^d}^\mathsf{H}\mathbf{H}_k\mathbf{P}_i\mathbf{P}_i^\mathsf{H}\mathbf{H}_k^\mathsf{H}\mathbf{U}_k^d+4\sigma_k^2{\mathbf{U}_k^d}^\mathsf{H} \mathbf{U}_k^d$. 
 Since the MiLACs are assumed to be lossless, they do not introduce any power dissipation. Accordingly, the proposed MiLAC transceiver and its fully digital counterpart are subject to the same radiated transmit-power budget, with the internal power-allocation variables governing the power distribution across the signal streams.

 Although problem~\eqref{eq:pro-formula-di} is nonconvex, it can be efficiently addressed using established alternating-optimization methods, such as fractional programming (FP) \cite{shen2018fractional} and weighted minimum mean-square error (WMMSE) optimization \cite{WMMSE2011,rethingkingWMMSE}. In this work, we adopt WMMSE to obtain a stationary solution $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm d}^{\star}$ and the corresponding MMSE combiners $\{\mathbf{U}_{k}^{\mathrm d,\star}\}_{k=1}^{K}$.
The closed-form constructions in Theorems~\ref{the:MiLAC-HBF} and~\ref{the:opt-1MiLAC} are then applied to realize the same transmit beamformer and receive-combining performance using the proposed MiLAC-aided architecture. 

\section{Simulation}\label{sec:sim}
In this section, we evaluate the sum-rate performance of the proposed transceiver architecture incorporating a two-layer MiLAC transmitter, using the algorithm developed in Section~\ref{sec:pro-alg}. We compare its performance with baseline architectures to validate the optimality results established in Section~\ref{sec:optimality}.
We consider independent Rayleigh fading channels, where the entries of each channel matrix
$\mathbf H_k\in\mathbb C^{N_k\times L}$ for user-$k$ are independently drawn from $\mathcal{CN}(0,1)$. The noise variance is normalized to $\sigma_k^2=1$ for each user, and the transmit SNR is defined as $\mathrm{SNR}
	=
	10\log_{10}(\frac{P_t}{\sigma_k^2})\ \mathrm{dB}$.
The relative sum-rate performance tolerance is set to $10^{-5}$, and the iterative algorithms terminate when this criterion is satisfied. The results are averaged over $100$ independent channel realizations.
The following five transceiver architectures are compared:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \emph{Fully digital}: Both the transmit precoder and receive combiners are implemented digitally and jointly optimized using WMMSE, with closed-form MMSE receive-combiner updates \cite{WMMSE2011}. 

	\item \emph{MiLAC HBF}: This architecture employs hybrid digital-MiLAC beamforming at the transmitter and one-layer MiLAC combining at each receiver. The fully digital transmit precoder is factorized via SVD and exactly reconstructed through cascaded digital and MiLAC processing stages~\cite{limits-milac}.


	\item \emph{One-layer MiLAC}: One-layer MiLAC architecture implements beamforming at the transmitter and each receiver. The transmit and receive beamforming matrices, together with the transmit power allocation, are jointly optimized using the WMMSE-PGD algorithm \cite{limits-milac} under the corresponding spectral-norm and power constraints. 

	\item \emph{PS HBF}: Phase-shifter-based hybrid architecture implements beamforming at the transmitter and receivers, using $D$ transmit RF chains and $D_k$ receive RF chains at each receiver. The transmit beamformer is updated using PSLA-based algorithm \cite{two-layer-con} under constant-modulus constraints on its RF matrix. Each receive RF matrix is obtained by elementwise phase projection of the corresponding unconstrained MMSE combiner, after which the digital MMSE combiner is recomputed. The transmit and receive updates are performed alternately.

	\item \emph{Proposed transceiver}: The transmitter employs two-layer MiLAC architecture, while each receiver employs one-layer MiLAC architecture. The fully digital transmit precoder is exactly reproduced through the SVD-based construction for two-layer MiLAC architecture. At user-$k$, the fully digital MMSE combiner is uniformly scaled to satisfy the one-layer MiLAC spectral-norm constraint.
\end{enumerate}
