	\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed a two-layer MiLAC architecture to overcome the performance limitations of one-layer MiLAC transmitters in downlink multi-user MIMO networks. Using multiport microwave network theory, we established a general physics-compliant model that captures component interactions and derived a simplified model for unilateral and perfectly matched amplifiers. Under this simplified model with lossless and reciprocal MiLACs, we proved that the two-layer transmitter can realize any fully digital transmit beamformer, while a single-layer MiLAC receiver can achieve the same rate as fully digital linear combining. We derived closed-form mappings to the corresponding MiLAC implementations and developed a sum-rate maximization framework to jointly design the power allocation and scattering matrices. Numerical results confirmed that the proposed transceiver matches the sum-rate performance of its fully digital counterpart under the considered system model. These findings establish the potential of combining a two-layer MiLAC transmitter with one-layer MiLAC receivers to realize fully digital beamforming performance through analog-domain processing.


