 \section{Proof for Theorem \ref{the:MiLAC-HBF}}\label{app:proof-theorem1}
Following the notation in \eqref{eq:Theta-partition}, let $M_1=2D$ and $M_2=L+D$. For the two-layer MiLAC architecture proposed in Section \ref{sec:proposed-architecture}, we obtain its effective beamformer $ \mathbf{G} \in \mathbb{C}^{L \times D}$ at the transmitter as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:eff-G}
	\mathbf{G}=\mathbf{W}\mathbf{P}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathbf{F} = \bm \Theta_{2,21}\mathbf{S}_{A,21}\bm\Theta_{1,21}.
\end{equation}
The second equality in \eqref{eq:eff-G} is obtained by substituting \eqref{eq:theta1}, \eqref{eq:theta2} and \eqref{eq:amplifier-gain} into \eqref{eq:eff-G}. To prove Theorem~\ref{the:MiLAC-HBF}, it is sufficient to show that there exists a matrix $\mathbf{G}$ satisfying the constraints of the MiLAC architecture and is equivalent to the fully digital beamformer 
$\mathbf{P}_d$. This can be established by demonstrating that the following optimization problem always admits a global solution with an objective value of zero:
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:ls}
	\begin{align}
		\min_{\bm\Theta_1,\bm \Theta_2,\mathbf{P}^{\frac{1}{2}}} \quad& 
		\left\|\mathbf{P}_d-\bm\Theta_{2,21}\mathbf{S}_{A,21}\bm\Theta_{1,21}\right\|_\mathsf{F}^2 \label{eq:ls-obj}
		\text{s.t.} \quad 
		& \eqref{eq:amplifier-gain},\eqref{eq:con-theta-m},\eqref{eq:con-power}.
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
To solve problem \eqref{eq:ls}, we first consider the SVD of $\mathbf{P}_d$ in \eqref{eq:Pd-SVD}. Since columns of $\mathbf{P}_d$ lie in $\operatorname{span}(\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1)$, it is natural to choose $\bm \Theta_{2,21}^\star = \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1$. For a fixed $\bm \Theta_{2,21}^\star$,  the objective in \eqref{eq:ls-obj} is minimized by choosing $\bm\Theta_{1,21}^\star = \overline{\mathbf{V}}^\mathsf{H}$. 
However, $\bm \Theta_{2,21}^\star$ and $\bm\Theta_{1,21}^\star$ are only sub-blocks of $\bm \Theta_2^\star$ and $\bm\Theta_1^\star$, respectively. We must therefore construct full matrices $\bm \Theta_2^\star$ and $\bm\Theta_1^\star$ that satisfy the constraints \eqref{eq:con-theta-m} in \eqref{eq:ls}. To this end, we begin by verifying the feasibility of $\bm \Theta_2$, followed by that of $\bm \Theta_1$. To ensure that the structural constraints in \eqref{eq:theta2} and \eqref{eq:con-theta-m} are satisfied, the feasibility of $\bm \Theta_2$ can be assessed by solving the following problem:
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:feasible-check}
	\begin{align}
		\text{find}\quad &\bm \Theta_{2,11},\bm \Theta_{2,22}
		\text{s.t.}\quad &
		\begin{bmatrix}
 \bm \Theta_{2,11} & \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^\mathsf{T}
 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1 & \bm \Theta_{2,22}
		\end{bmatrix}^\mathsf{H}
		\begin{bmatrix}
 \bm \Theta_{2,11} & \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^\mathsf{T}
 \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1 & \bm \Theta_{2,22}
		\end{bmatrix}
		=\mathbf{I}_{L+D}, \label{eq:unit-norm}
		&
		\bm \Theta_{2,11}=\bm \Theta_{2,11}^\mathsf{T},
		&
		\bm \Theta_{2,22}=\bm \Theta_{2,22}^\mathsf{T}.
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Since $\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^\mathsf{H}\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1=\mathbf{I}_D$, the first $D$ columns of $\bm \Theta_2$ must have unit $\ell_2$ norm. This forces $\bm \Theta_{2,11}^\star = \mathbf{0}_{D\times D}$.
The remaining feasibility conditions in \eqref{eq:feasible-check} reduce to
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:phi22}
	\begin{align}
		\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^\mathsf{H} \bm \Theta_{2,22} &= \mathbf{0}_{D\times L},
		\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^*\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1^\mathsf{T} + \bm \Theta_{2,22}^\mathsf{H}\bm \Theta_{2,22} &= \mathbf{I}_L,
		\bm \Theta_{2,22} &= \bm \Theta_{2,22}^\mathsf{T}.
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
A valid solution for $\bm \Theta_{2,22}$ is $\bm \Theta_{2,22}^\star = -\overline{\mathbf{U}}_2\overline{\mathbf{U}}_2^\mathsf{T}$,
which satisfies all three constraints in \eqref{eq:phi22}. Notably, the selection of $\bm \Theta_{2,22}$ has no impact on system performance. Accordingly, the optimal solution for MiLAC 2 is given by \eqref{eq:phi-star}.



Following the same procedure as that used to obtain $\bm \Theta_2^\star$ in \eqref{eq:feasible-check}-\eqref{eq:phi22}, we obtain the optimal solution for the scattering matrix $\bm \Theta_1$ of MiLAC 1 as \eqref{eq:theta-star}.



\par
Now, it remains to optimize $\mathbf{P}^{\frac{1}{2}}$ in \eqref{eq:ls}.
With $\bm \Theta_{2,21}^\star=\overline{\mathbf{U}}_1$ and $\bm\Theta_{1,21}^\star=\overline{\mathbf{V}}^\mathsf{H}$, problem \eqref{eq:ls} reduces to
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:ls-P}
	\begin{align}
		\min_{\mathbf{P}^{\frac{1}{2}}} \quad &
		\big\|\overline{\bm{\Sigma}} - \mathbf{S}_{A,21}\big\|_\mathsf{F}^2
		\text{s.t.}\quad&
		\eqref{eq:amplifier-gain},\eqref{eq:con-power}.
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Notice that \eqref{eq:ls-P} is a constrained least-squares problem. 
	Since the given fully digital beamformer satisfies the power constraint, $\overline{\bm\Sigma}$ is
	feasible for problem \eqref{eq:ls-P}. Therefore, the global minimum is zero, attained at	$\mathbf S_{A,21}^{\star}=\overline{\bm\Sigma}$,
	which gives \eqref{eq:p-star}.
In summary, under the solution $(\bm\Theta_1^\star,\bm\Theta_2^\star,(\mathbf{P}^\star)^{\frac{1}{2}})$, the objective value of \eqref{eq:ls} is zero, and this solution satisfies
\begin{equation}
	\bm\Theta^\star_{2,21}\mathbf{S}_{A,21}^\star\bm\Theta^\star_{1,21}
	= \overline{\mathbf{U}}_1\overline{\bm{\Sigma}}\;\overline{\mathbf{V}}^\mathsf{H}
	= \mathbf{P}_d,
\end{equation}
which proves that two-layer MiLAC-aided beamforming can always reproduce the fully digital beamformer when both MiLAC 1 and MiLAC 2 are lossless and reciprocal. Therefore, we complete the proof.
\hfill$\square$
\section{Proof for Theorem \ref{the:opt-1MiLAC}}\label{app:proof-theorem2}
For analytical convenience, we normalize the received signal in \eqref{eq:re-signal} as
$\overline{\mathbf y}_k=2\mathbf y_k
=\sum_{i=1}^{K}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_i\mathbf s_i
+\overline{\mathbf n}_k$,
where $\overline{\mathbf n}_k=2\mathbf n_k
\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf 0,4\sigma_k^2\mathbf I_{N_k})$.
Since this nonzero scaling does not affect the
achievable rate, we use $\overline{\mathbf y}_k$
for the following MMSE analysis.
For user $k$, define the received-signal covariance matrix and the
	interference-plus-noise covariance matrix, respectively, as
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf R_{y,k}
		&=
		\sum_{i=1}^{K}
		\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_i\mathbf G_i^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		+4\sigma_k^2\mathbf I_{N_k},
		\label{eq:received_covariance}
		\mathbf R_{-k}
		&=
		\sum_{\substack{i=1i\neq k}}^{K}
		\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_i\mathbf G_i^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		+4\sigma_k^2\mathbf I_{N_k}.
		\label{eq:interference_covariance}
	\end{align}
	Accordingly, $\mathbf R_{y,k}
	=
	\mathbf R_{-k}
	+
	\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k\mathbf G_k^{\mathsf H}
	\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}$. Since $\sigma_k^2>0$, both $\mathbf R_{y,k}$ and
	$\mathbf R_{-k}$ are positive definite.

	For a fully digital receive combining matrix
	$\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}\in\mathbb C^{N_k\times D_k}$, the estimate
	of $\mathbf s_k$ is given by $\widehat{\mathbf s}_k
		=
		(\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d})^{\mathsf H}\overline{\mathbf y}_k$. The corresponding MSE matrix is
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf E_k
		={}&
		\mathbb E\left[
		(\widehat{\mathbf s}_k-\mathbf s_k)
		(\widehat{\mathbf s}_k-\mathbf s_k)^{\mathsf H}
		\right] \notag
		={}&
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		-(\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d})^{\mathsf H}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k
		-\mathbf G_k^{\mathsf H}\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d} +
		(\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d})^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf R_{y,k}\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}.
		\label{eq:rx_mse_matrix}
	\end{align}
	Differentiating $\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf E_k)$ with respect to
	$(\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d})^*$ gives
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\partial\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf E_k)}
		{\partial(\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d})^*}
		=
		\mathbf R_{y,k}\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm d}
		-\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k.
	\end{equation}
	Therefore, the unique linear MMSE combiner is
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:fd_mmse_receiver}
		\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}
		=
		\mathbf R_{y,k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k.
	\end{equation}

	Substituting \eqref{eq:fd_mmse_receiver} into
	\eqref{eq:rx_mse_matrix} yields
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf E_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}
		&=
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		-
		\mathbf G_k^{\mathsf H}\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf R_{y,k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k 
		&=
		\left(
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		+
		\mathbf G_k^{\mathsf H}\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf R_{-k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k
		\right)^{-1},
		\label{eq:mmse_error_rate_relation}
	\end{align}
	where the second equality follows from the matrix inversion
	lemma. Consequently, the rate attained by the MMSE combiner is
	\begin{align}
		R_k^{\mathrm d}
		&=
		-\log_2\det(\mathbf E_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}) 
		&=
		\log_2\det\left(
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		+
		\mathbf G_k^{\mathsf H}\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf R_{-k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k
		\right).
		\label{eq:fd_mmse_rate}
	\end{align}

	To verify that this is the maximum rate attainable by a linear
	fully digital combiner, the matrix inversion lemma also gives
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}
		={}&
		\mathbf R_{-k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k
		\left(
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		+
		\mathbf G_k^{\mathsf H}\mathbf H_k^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf R_{-k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k
		\right)^{-1}.
		\label{eq:mmse_rate_optimal_form}
	\end{align}
	The rightmost matrix in \eqref{eq:mmse_rate_optimal_form} is
	nonsingular. Hence, the MMSE combiner and
	$\mathbf R_{-k}^{-1}\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k$ span the same
	receive-combining subspace and differ only by a nonsingular
	right-side transformation. Since such a transformation does not
	change the achievable rate, \eqref{eq:fd_mmse_rate} is the maximum
	rate attainable by a fully digital linear combiner.

	Next, define the positive scaling factor
	\begin{equation}
		c_k
		=
		\min\left\{
		1,\,
		\frac{1}
		{2\|\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}\|_2}
		\right\}
		\label{eq:rx_scaling_factor}
	\end{equation}
	and the scaled and conjugated MMSE combiner
	\begin{equation}
		\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k
		=
		c_k\left(\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}\right)^*.
		\label{eq:scaled_mmse_receiver}
	\end{equation}
	The assumption
	$\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k)=D_k$ ensures that
	$\|\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}\|_2>0$ and, hence,
	$c_k>0$. By construction,
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:rx_contraction}
		\|\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k\|_2
		\leq
		\frac{1}{2}.
	\end{equation}

	We next show that any matrix satisfying
	\eqref{eq:rx_contraction} can be implemented by a single-layer MiLAC.
	Consider the full SVD
	\begin{equation}
		2\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k
		=
		\overline{\mathbf Q}_k\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k\overline{\mathbf P}_k^{\mathsf H},
		\label{eq:scaled_combiner_svd}
	\end{equation}
	where $\overline{\mathbf Q}_k\in\mathbb C^{N_k\times N_k}$ and
	$\overline{\mathbf P}_k\in\mathbb C^{D_k\times D_k}$ are unitary, while
	$\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k\in\mathbb R^{N_k\times D_k}$ is rectangular
	diagonal. It follows from \eqref{eq:rx_contraction} that
	$\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k\|_2\leq1$.

	Define
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf C_{D,k}
		&=
		\left(
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		-\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{\mathsf T}
		\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k
		\right)^{1/2}, 
		\mathbf C_{N,k}
		&=
		\left(
		\mathbf I_{N_k}
		-\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k
		\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{\mathsf T}
		\right)^{1/2}.
	\end{align}
	These matrices are well defined because all singular values in
	$\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k$ are no greater than one. Now construct
	\begin{equation}
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}
		=
		\begin{bmatrix}
 \mathbf C_{D,k}
 &
 \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{\mathsf T}

 \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k
 &
 -\mathbf C_{N,k}
		\end{bmatrix}.
		\label{eq:canonical_unitary_completion}
	\end{equation}
	Clearly,
	$\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}
	=\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}^{\mathsf T}$. Moreover, the definitions
	of $\mathbf C_{D,k}$ and $\mathbf C_{N,k}$ imply
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbf C_{N,k}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k
		=
		\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k\mathbf C_{D,k}.
	\end{equation}
	It then follows by direct block multiplication that
	\begin{equation}
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}^{\mathsf H}
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}
		=
		\mathbf I_{N_k+D_k}.
	\end{equation}
	Thus, $\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}$ is both symmetric and unitary.

	Let
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbf T_k
		=
		\operatorname{blkdiag}
		\left(\overline{\mathbf P}_k^*,\overline{\mathbf Q}_k\right)
	\end{equation}
	and define
	\begin{equation}
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k
		=
		\mathbf T_k
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_{0,k}
		\mathbf T_k^{\mathsf T}.
		\label{eq:rx_scattering_completion}
	\end{equation}
	Since $\mathbf T_k$ is unitary, the unitary congruence
	transformation in \eqref{eq:rx_scattering_completion} preserves
	both symmetry and unitarity. Therefore,
	\begin{equation}
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k
		=
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k^{\mathsf T},
		\qquad
		\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k^{\mathsf H}\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k
		=
		\mathbf I_{N_k+D_k}.
	\end{equation}
	Furthermore, its lower-left block satisfies
	\begin{align}
		[\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k]_
		{D_k+1:D_k+N_k,\,1:D_k}
		&=
		\overline{\mathbf Q}_k\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k\overline{\mathbf P}_k^{\mathsf H} 
		&=
		2\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k.
	\end{align}
	Hence, the corresponding MiLAC combining matrix is
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm m}
		=
		\frac{1}{2}
		[\boldsymbol{\Psi}_k]_
		{D_k+1:D_k+N_k,\,1:D_k}
		=
		\widetilde{\mathbf U}_k.
		\label{eq:milac_scaled_mmse}
	\end{equation}
	Therefore, the scaled and conjugated fully digital MMSE combiner can be exactly
	realized by a single-layer MiLAC.

	It remains to show that the scaling in
	\eqref{eq:scaled_mmse_receiver} does not affect the achievable
	rate. Define the effective desired-signal matrix and the
	post-combining interference-plus-noise covariance matrix of the
	fully digital MMSE combiner as
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm d}
		&=
		(\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d})^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf H_k\mathbf G_k, 
		\mathbf J_k^{\mathrm d}
		&=
		(\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d})^{\mathsf H}
		\mathbf R_{-k}
		\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}.
	\end{align}
	Since
	$\mathbf U_k^{\mathrm m}
	=c_k\left(\mathbf U_{k,\mathrm{MMSE}}^{\mathrm d}\right)^*$, the corresponding
	MiLAC quantities satisfy
	\begin{align}
		\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm m}
		&=
		c_k\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm d}, 
		\mathbf J_k^{\mathrm m}
		&=
		|c_k|^2\mathbf J_k^{\mathrm d}.
	\end{align}
	Because $c_k\neq0$, it follows that
	\begin{align}
		R_k^{\mathrm m}
		&=
		\log_2\det\left(
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		+
		(\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm m})^{\mathsf H}
		(\mathbf J_k^{\mathrm m})^{-1}
		\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm m}
		\right) 
		&=
		\log_2\det\left(
		\mathbf I_{D_k}
		+
		(\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm d})^{\mathsf H}
		(\mathbf J_k^{\mathrm d})^{-1}
		\mathbf S_k^{\mathrm d}
		\right) 
		&=
		R_k^{\mathrm d}.
		\label{eq:receiver_rate_equivalence}
	\end{align}
	Thus, uniform nonzero scaling of the receive combiner scales the
	desired signal, interference, and post-combining noise by
	corresponding common factors, which cancel in the achievable-rate
	expression.

	Finally, the construction above maps a fully digital MMSE combiner to a feasible one-layer MiLAC combiner that attains exactly the same rate. Hence, the two receiver architectures achieve the same achievable rate for every user and, consequently, the same maximum sum-rate.
