\begin{abstract}
Driven by the ever-growing demand for intelligent maritime services, cooperative unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) and unmanned surface vehicle (USV) operations, together with wireless integrated sensing, communication, and computation (ISCC), have become pivotal in maritime environments. However, the distinctive maritime environment precludes a direct application of terrestrial
ISCC models, while sensing, communication, and computation compete for the same spatio-temporal resources, thus yielding inherent performance trade-offs that become particularly severe in
resource-constrained ocean scenarios. To address these issues, we investigate an ISCC system in which multiple UAVs detect targets at the sea surface and partially offload the collected data to a USV for cooperative processing. Characterizing the maritime-specific environment impairments, we incorporate sea clutter and wave-induced USV roll motion into the sensing and communication models, respectively. Moreover, we introduce data queueing models at both the UAVs and the USV to capture the dynamic coupling among sensing-data generation, wireless offloading, and computation execution. The optimization objective is to minimize the long-term average system energy consumption by jointly optimizing UAV–target association, UAV trajectories, beamforming, time allocation, offloading ratios, and computation resource allocation. Given that these decisions differ in update frequency and time sensitivity, we propose a Lyapunov-guided dual-timescale hierarchical soft actor-critic (LTH-SAC) framework, which transforms the long-term problem into a sequence of queue-aware online subproblems and coordinates optimization across the two timescales. Simulation results verify the effectiveness of the proposed framework and demonstrate its capability to balance the stability-performance trade-off among sensing, communication, and computation.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Integrated sensing, communication, and computation (ISCC), unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV), unmanned surface vehicle (USV), hierarchical reinforcement learning. 
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
The rapid development of intelligent maritime services, such as ocean monitoring, ship navigation, and offshore inspection,
is driving an increasing demand for ubiquitous perception, reliable communication, and prompt data processing\cite{FSAlqurashi2023}. However, these functionalities are usually designed separately in conventional maritime networks, making it difficult to simultaneously guarantee relevant quality-of-service (QoS) in harsh maritime environments with limited resources and stringent energy budgets. The recent advancement of integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) \cite{FLiu2022,FLiu2020,SLiu2020} and multi-access edge computing (MEC)\cite{Mach2017Mobile,Mao2017MEC,Lin2024Maritime} have paved the way for a broader paradigm, namely integrated sensing, communication, and computation (ISCC), which establishes an end-to-end information processing framework from data acquisition and transmission to edge task execution \cite{Wen2025Survey,CLi2025,WChen2026}. 
Nevertheless, constrained by the fixed deployment, conventional terrestrial infrastructures can hardly cover vast open-sea areas, thereby struggling to adaptively provide high performance ISCC to the sparse and time-varying distribution of maritime users. In addition, constructing offshore facilities together with backhaul links inevitably causes prohibitive costs. Due to the high maneuverability,
on-demand deployment, and predominant line-of-sight (LoS) channels, unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) are envisioned as promising ISCC platforms that provide wide-area coverage along with flexible sensing,
communication, and computing capabilities for maritime
services~\cite{Meng2024UAVEnabled,Zhou2018Computation,Liu2023MLTrajectory}. In parallel, unmanned surface vehicles (USVs) are free from the stringent payload and endurance constraints of airborne platforms, and thus are equipped with relatively powerful computing resources.  Operating as maritime edge nodes, the USV supports information aggregation and on-site decision making, thereby serving as a complement to UAVs in accomplishing complex maritime ISCC tasks~\cite{ZWang2024,RZhang2026}.
Despite the above advantages, the design of the UAV-USV collaborative maritime ISCC system poses unique challenges. On the one hand, {\em the maritime environment invalidates several models adopted in terrestrial ISCC designs}. Specifically, maritime target sensing is impaired by sea clutter scattered from the irregular
sea surface, which changes the statistical characteristics of received echoes and degrades sensing performance~\cite{CarreteroMoya2010SeaClutter}. Moreover, the air-to-sea (A2S) channels in open-sea environments are jointly shaped by the LoS path and sea-surface reflection, which is significantly different from terrestrial links~\cite{Nomikos2023Survey}. Finally, the wave-induced vessel motion perturbs signal transmission and reception. In particular, the roll motion of USVs continuously rotates the onboard antenna array, varying the effective angle of arrival (AoA) and beam alignment~\cite{Soltani2011ShipAntenna}. On the other hand, {\em sensing, communication, and computation compete for the same
spatio-temporal resources, leading to inherent performance trade-offs}. Allocating more time to sensing improves information acquisition and estimation accuracy, yet generates more data to be transmitted and processed. Similarly, increasing computation offloading alleviates the local processing burden of UAVs, but imposes additional pressure on the A2S links and the computing resources of USVs. Consequently, persistent data backlogs may accumulate and eventually interrupt long-endurance missions, which is particularly severe in resource-constrained ocean scenarios. Therefore, for resource-constrained UAV-USV collaborative maritime ISCC systems, it is crucial to
jointly model the queue dynamics at both the UAV and the USV, so as to capture the interactions among sensing-data generation, communication offloading, and computation execution, thereby balancing system performance, energy consumption, and queue stability.
\subsection{Related Works}
Recent studies have investigated the integration of sensing, communication, and computation in UAV-enabled networks. For example, the authors in \cite{PHou2025} developed a distributed deep reinforcement learning framework for cooperative UAV-enabled ISCC systems, jointly optimizing bandwidth allocation, transmit power, and computing capacity under sensing and latency constraints. For multi-UAV-assisted ISCC systems, \cite{Deng2024MultiUAVISCC} jointly optimized user association, target assignment, deep neural network (DNN) splitting, beamforming, computation resource allocation, and UAV locations to meet latency and accuracy requirements. In \cite{Zhou2024UAVISCC}, UAV CPU frequency, sensing power, user transmit power, and trajectory were jointly optimized to minimize weighted energy consumption. Similarly, \cite{Chen2024UAVISCC} jointly optimized UAV trajectory, beamforming, and computation offloading to improve computing efficiency while satisfying sensing-quality requirements.
However, most existing UAV-assisted ISCC designs were developed for terrestrial or generic air-to-ground scenarios and do not properly capture maritime-specific propagation and platform dynamics. 
Unlike terrestrial environments, maritime environments exhibit unique propagation and sensing characteristics. In \cite{Wang2018Wireless}, the authors investigated maritime wireless channels and highlighted distinctive features such as sparse scattering and sea-wave-induced channel variations. In \cite{Zhang2025U2SChannel}, a geometry-based UAV-to-ship channel model was developed by explicitly incorporating line-of-sight propagation, sea-surface reflection, and ship oscillation, demonstrating the significant impact of maritime dynamics on channel non-stationarity. From the sensing perspective, \cite{NAhmed2025} incorporated sea clutter and wave shadowing into a non-stationary UAV-to-ship multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) channel model to characterize their effects on received signals. Moreover, \cite{JYu2024} demonstrated that wave-induced rotational motion of USVs, particularly rolling, can cause severe beam misalignment and consequently degrade wireless transmission performance. 
Although maritime integrated wireless systems have attracted increasing attention, existing studies remain relatively limited and predominantly focus on ISAC. In \cite{Zhang2024MaritimeISAC}, the authors developed a shipboard maritime ISAC system and jointly optimized system deployment and transmission parameters to improve communication performance while satisfying sensing requirements. In \cite{Zhang2024MaritimeResource}, the authors investigated multi-user maritime ISAC resource allocation and revealed the trade-off between communication and sensing performance under different sensing requirements. For maritime emergency networks, \cite{Li2025MaritimeISAC} jointly optimized UAV trajectory, subcarrier assignment, and power allocation to maximize end-to-end communication rate while satisfying sensing QoS constraints.
At the broader network-architecture level, \cite{Dai2022SmartOceans} surveyed ISCC networks for smart oceans, discussing the collaboration of space, aerial, sea-surface, and underwater layers, while dedicated studies on joint optimization of maritime ISCC systems remain limited. The incorporation of computation further complicates resource coordination because sensing, communication, and computation compete for limited time, transmit power, bandwidth, and computing resources. \cite{Wen2025Survey} reviewed the interdependence among these functionalities and the resulting resource competition. Accordingly, several studies have investigated performance trade-offs in ISCC systems. \cite{Xu2023UAVISCC} characterized the trade-off between computation capacity and sensing beampattern gain in UAV-enabled ISCC systems, showing that improving one may compromise the other. Similarly, \cite{Liu2025ISCCOffloading} jointly optimized transmit beamforming and computation offloading in a three-tier ISCC framework to reduce task execution latency while satisfying sensing constraints.
To characterize dynamic performance trade-offs, queueing models and queue-aware resource management have been explored in MEC and ISAC systems.
In \cite{Zhang2019UAVMEC}, the authors modeled stochastic task arrivals using data queues and employed Lyapunov optimization to jointly optimize offloading, resource allocation, and UAV trajectory while ensuring queue stability. 
For maritime internet of things (IoT)-MEC systems, \cite{Bai2025Dynamic} developed a Lyapunov-based dynamic offloading scheme to balance energy consumption and delay while accounting for stochastic arrivals and queue stability. 
In \cite{Gao2026ISCCQueue}, the authors incorporated sensing-data offloading and queue stability into a Lyapunov drift-plus-penalty framework for online resource management. For maritime UAV-ISAC networks, \cite{Li2026MaritimeISACQueue} investigated queue-aware data collection by modeling buffered sensing observations at maritime buoys and coordinating sensing, communication, and UAV mobility. In underwater acoustic sensor networks, \cite{Gou2026UnderwaterISACQueue} incorporated AoI-related queue states into multi-agent ISAC optimization to balance information freshness and communication throughput. Despite these advances, the joint queue dynamics of sensing, communication, and computation functionalities in maritime ISCC systems remain under-explored. In particular, the coupling among sensing data generation, UAV-side offloading queues, and USV-side computation queues, as well as its impacts on long-term stability and energy-performance trade-offs under maritime-specific conditions, have not been adequately characterized.
\subsection{Main Contributions}
Inspired by the above discussions, this paper investigates a multi-UAV-assisted maritime ISCC network, where ISAC-enabled UAVs sense maritime targets, locally process sensing data, and offload remaining tasks to a USV for further computation and fusion. The objective is to minimize the long-term average energy consumption under sensing, computation, mobility, and queue-stability constraints. To solve the resulting coupled stochastic optimization problem, we develop a Lyapunov-guided dual-timescale hierarchical soft actor-critic (LTH-SAC) framework for joint long-term service planning and short-term resource allocation. The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item We consider a maritime ISCC framework that captures the impacts of complex maritime environments and the coupling among sensing, communication, and computation. In particular, sea clutter is incorporated into the sensing model, while maritime channel characteristics and USV roll motion are considered in the communication model to characterize the dynamic variations of performance. Moreover, UAV-side and USV-side queues are introduced to capture the end-to-end data flow from sensing-data generation to wireless offloading and computation processing, revealing the stability-performance trade-off.

 \item We formulate a dual-timescale long-term stochastic optimization problem to balance system energy consumption, sensing performance, communication efficiency, and queue stability. By jointly optimizing UAV-target association, trajectory planning, beamforming, time allocation, computation offloading, and computing resource allocation, the proposed formulation characterizes the stability–performance trade-off. Considering the heterogeneous update frequencies and time sensitivities of different decisions, a dual-timescale framework is introduced, where large-timescale service planning and small-timescale resource adaptation are coordinated through Lyapunov drift-plus-penalty optimization.

 \item We propose a LTH-SAC algorithm to solve the resulting mixed-integer and highly coupled optimization problem. The high-level hybrid SAC policy determines long-term decisions including UAV-target association and trajectory planning, while the low-level multi-agent SAC (MASAC) policy performs adaptive resource allocation. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves superior energy efficiency and queue stability compared with benchmark algorithms, while revealing and effectively balancing the inherent trade-offs among sensing, communication, computation, and system stability.

\end{enumerate}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{sec:model} presents the considered multi-UAV-assisted maritime ISCC system model and problem formulation. Section \ref{sec:problem} applies Lyapunov optimization to derive a slot-wise drift-plus-penalty problem. Section \ref{sec:algorithm} introduces the proposed LTH-SAC algorithm. Section \ref{sec:results} presents the simulation results, and finally Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
\section{System Model}
\label{sec:model}
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig1}, we consider a multi-UAV-assisted maritime ISCC network, which consists of a USV, \(K\) maritime targets,
and $U$ UAVs equipped with a uniform linear array of $N_{u,t}$ transmit antennas and $N_{u,r}$ receive antennas, 
which are defined as the sets  $\mathcal{K} =\{1,2,\cdots,K\}$ and $\mathcal{U}=\{1,2,\cdots,U\}$. 
Each UAV is equipped with an ISAC device and a MEC server to perform radar sensing, communication and
computing. The UAVs first sense maritime targets and generate sensing data. Due to the limited onboard computation capability, the sensed data are partially processed locally by UAVs and partially offloaded to the USV for further computation and fusion. To characterize the different variation rates of long-term service decisions and delay-sensitive resource allocation, we introduce a two-timescale framework, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig2}. Specifically, the flight duration of the UAVs is divided into $C$ large-timescale frames. The set of frame indices is denoted by $\mathcal{C} \equiv \{1, \cdots, C\}$, and we use $t_c$ to indicate the $c$-th frame. Each frame can be
divided into multiple small time slots, which are  indexed by $n \in \mathcal{N} \equiv \{1, \cdots, N\}$. The $n$-th small time slot in frame $c$ is denoted by $t_c^n$, and the duration of each slot is $\delta_t$. Accordingly, the duration of each frame is $T_c=N\delta_t$, and the total flight duration is $T=C N\delta_t$.
