We consider a three-dimensional (3D) Cartesian coordinate system, where the coordinates of the USV and target $k$ in frame $t_c$ are denoted by $\mathbf{q}_0[t_c]=(x_0[t_c],y_0[t_c],0)^T$ and $\mathbf{g}_k [ t_c ]=(x_k[t_c], y_k[t_c], 0)^{T}$. We consider that the waypoint of UAV $u$ at the beginning of frame $t_c$ is $\mathbf{q}_u [ t_c ]=(x_u [ t_c ], y_u[ t_c ],  H_u )^{T}$ with $H$ being the fixed altitude of each UAV, and the flight speed of UAV $u$ can be expressed as
$\mathbf v_u [t_c^n]
=
\frac{
	\mathbf q_u^H[t_{c+1}]-\mathbf q_u^H[t_c]
}
{N\delta_t}.$ 
In addition, we introduce a large-timescale binary variable $\alpha_{u, k} [ t_c ] \in\{0, 1 \} $ that indicates whether target $k$ is assigned to UAV $u$ during frame $t_c$. 
In order to improve the resource utilization efficiency, each target is assigned to at most one UAV \cite{PHou2025}, and each UAV only senses at most $K_u^{\rm max}$ targets due to hardware limitations, i.e., 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{u=1}^{U} \alpha_{u, k} [ t_c ] \leq1, \sum_{k=1}^{K} \alpha_{u, k} [ t_c ] \leq K_u^{\rm max}, \forall k \in \mathcal{K}, u \in \mathcal{U}, \forall t_c.
\end{equation}
Moreover, let $\lambda_u[t_c^n]\in[0,1]$ denote the offloading ratio of UAV $u$, while the remaining fraction $1-\lambda_u[t_c^n]$ is processed locally by UAV $u$.
To avoid interference, time division duplexing (TDD) is employed to perform radar sensing and communication. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig2}, each time slot $t_c^n$ is further divided into two sub-slots. For UAV \( u \), the first sub-time slot $\xi_{u} [ t_c^n ] \delta_{t} $ is dedicated to target sensing, while the second sub-time slot $( 1-\xi_{u} [ t_c^n ] ) \delta_{t} $ is allocated for task offloading to the USV, where $\xi_{u} [ t_c^n ]\in{[0,1]}$ is the time slot allocation factor. After each sensing sub-time slot, local computation and task offloading are performed in parallel during the communication sub-slot. Furthermore, local computation in the current slot is assumed to be completed within the communication duration.
\subsection{Radar Sensing Model}
In the time slot $t_c^n$, each UAV first performs sensing to detect the targets on the sea surface. Let $s_{u,r}[t_c^n]$ denote the radar waveform emitted from the UAV $u$ with unit power, then the corresponding transmit signal can be expressed as
\begin{align}
\mathbf{x}_u[t_c^n]=\mathbf{p}_u[t_c^n]s_{u,r}[t_c^n],
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{p}_u[t_c^n]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{u,t}\times 1}$ is the transmit beamforming vector.
Unlike terrestrial target detection, sea clutter is a non-negligible factor for maritime target detection. Specifically, sea clutter can be characterized as a composite Gaussian model from the perspective of physical scattering and mathematical tractability, which is represented as a spherically invariant random vector (SIRV), i.e., a product of two linearly independent components that reflect the roughness of the sea surface and can be expressed as\cite{Zhang2024MaritimeISAC}
\begin{equation}
	{\bf{c}}_{u} [t_c^n]=\sqrt{\tau} \mathbf{z}_u[t_c^n],
\end{equation}
where $\tau$ denotes the texture component, which generally remains constant in a coherent processing interval (CPI) and represents the macroscopic structure formed by long gravity waves. $\mathbf{z}_u[t_c^n]\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}_{u,z} [t_c^n] \mathbf{I}_{N_{u,r}})$ denotes the speckle component that represents the microscopic structure formed by capillary waves.
\begin{figure}[t]
	\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, trim=30pt 18pt 10pt 50pt, clip]{./frame-slot_fix1.pdf}
	\centering
	\caption{\small Frame and slot structure of the maritime ISCC system.}\label{fig2}
	\vspace{-1mm}
\end{figure}
Then, the sensing echo received by UAV $u$ in time slot $t_c^n$ is
\begin{align}
&\mathbf{r}_{u} [ t_c^n ]=\sum_{k=1}^{K} \alpha_{u, k} [ t_c ] \beta_{u,k}[t_c^n] \mathbf{a} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{b}^{H} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{x}_u[t_c^n]\nonumber
&+\sum^{U}_{i=1,i\neq u}\sum^{K}_{l=1}\alpha_{i,l}[t_c]\beta_{i,l}[t_c^n] \mathbf{a}( \theta_{u,l} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{b}^{H} ( \theta_{i,l} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{x}_i[t_c^n]\nonumber
&+{\bf{c}}_{u} [t_c^n]+\mathbf{n}_{u,r} [ t_c^n ],
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{n}_{u,r} [t_c^n]\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf{0},\sigma^2_{u,r}[t_c^n]\mathbf{I}_{N_{u,r}})$ represents the additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN). 
$\beta_{u,k}[t_c^n]$ is the reflection coefficient of target $k$ for UAV $u$, which is modeled as
$
	\beta_{u,k}[t_c^n]
	=
	\frac{
		\sqrt{
 \bar{\sigma}_{k}(f_c,\mathbf{Q}_k)
 B^{\rm ang}_{u,k}[t_c^n]
 B^{\rm fluc}_{k}[t_c^n]
		}
	}{2d_{u,k}[t_c^n]}
	e^{j\psi_{u,k}[t_c^n]},
$
where $\bar{\sigma}_{k}(f_c,\mathbf{Q}_k)$ denotes the nominal radar cross section (RCS), which depends on carrier frequency $f_c$ and target geometry parameter $\mathbf{Q}_k$. 
$d_{u,k}[t_c^n]=\|\mathbf{q}_u[t_c^n]-\mathbf{g}_k[t_c]\|$ denotes the distance between UAV $u$ and target $k$ in time slot $t_c^n$. Other parameters are defined in \cite{chen2009mimo}.
$\mathbf{a}(\theta_{u,k}[t_c^n])$ and $\mathbf{b}(\theta_{u,k}[t_c^n])$ are the receive and transmit steering vectors, respectively, and can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
	\resizebox{1\hsize}{!}{$\begin{aligned}
 {\bf{a}}( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ])=[ 1, e^{j\pi \operatorname{s i n} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ])},\cdots, e^{j\pi \operatorname{s i n} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ]) ( N_{u,r}-1 )} ]^{T},
 {\bf{b}}( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ])=[ 1, e^{j\pi \operatorname{s i n} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ])},\cdots, e^{j\pi\operatorname{s i n} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ]) ( N_{u,t}-1 )} ]^{T},
		\end{aligned}$}
\end{equation}
where $\theta_{u,k}[t_c^n]=\arcsin\!\left(\frac{H}{\|\mathbf{q}_u[t_c^n]-\mathbf{g}_k[t_c]\|}\right)$ is the elevation angle from the target $k$ to UAV $u$. Let $\mathbf{A}(\theta_{u,k} [t_c^n])\triangleq\mathbf{a} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{b}^{H} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ] )$ and $\mathbf{B}( \theta_{u,l} [ t_c^n ],\theta_{i,l} [ t_c^n ] )\triangleq\mathbf{a} ( \theta_{u,l} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{b}^{H} ( \theta_{i,l} [ t_c^n ] )$.
$\mathbf{r}_{u}[t_c^n]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{u,r}\times 1}$ is filtered by a set of receive beamforming vectors $\{\mathbf{w}_{u,k}[t_c^n]\}$, and the corresponding output is given as
\begin{align}
&r_{u,k}[t_c^n]=\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]\mathbf{r}_{u}[t_c^n]\nonumber
&=\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]\sum_{k=1}^{K} \alpha_{u, k} [ t_c ] \beta_{u,k}[t_c^n] \mathbf{A} ( \theta_{u,k} [ t_c^n ] )\mathbf{x}_u[t_c^n]\nonumber
&+\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]\sum^{U}_{i=1,i\neq u}\sum^{K}_{l=1}\alpha_{i,l}[t_c]\beta_{i,l}[t_c^n] \mathbf{B}( \theta_{u,l} [ t_c^n ],\theta_{i,l} [ t_c^n ] ) \mathbf{x}_i[t_c^n]\nonumber
&+\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]{\bf{c}}_{u} [t_c^n]+\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]\mathbf{n}_{u,r} [ t_c^n ].
\end{align}
Then, the received SCNR at the UAV $u$ for the target $k$ at time slot $t_c^n$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}
\label{SCNR}
\gamma_{u,k}[t_c^n]
=\frac{\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]|\beta_{u,k}[t_c^n]|^2\left|\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]\mathbf d_{u,k}[t_c^n]\right|^2}{\mathbf{w}_{u,k}^{H}[t_c^n]\mathbf{\Psi}_{u,k}[t_c^n]\mathbf{w}_{u,k}[t_c^n]},
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
	\mathbf{\Psi}_{u,k}[t_c^n]
	=&\mathbf{R}_1[t_c^n]+\mathbf{R}_2[t_c^n]+(\sigma_{u,z}^2[t_c^n]+\sigma^2_{u,r}[t_c^n])\mathbf{I}_{N_{u,r}},
	\mathbf{R}_1[t_c^n]
	=& \sum_{\tilde{k}=1,\tilde{k}\neq k}^{K}
	\alpha_{u,\tilde{k}}[t_c]|\beta_{u,\tilde{k}}[t_c^n]|^2
	\mathbf{d}_{u,\tilde{k}}[t_c^n]
	\mathbf{d}_{u,\tilde{k}}^{H}[t_c^n],
	\mathbf{R}_2[t_c^n]
	=& \sum_{i=1,i\neq u}^{U}\sum_{l=1}^{K}
	\alpha_{i,l}[t_c]|\beta_{i,l}[t_c^n]|^2
	\mathbf{e}_{u,i,l}[t_c^n]
	\mathbf{e}_{u,i,l}^{H}[t_c^n],
\end{align}
and $\mathbf d_{u,k}[t_c^n]
\triangleq
\mathbf A(\theta_{u,k}[t_c^n])\mathbf p_u[t_c^n]$, $\mathbf{d}_{u,\tilde{k}}[t_c^n]
\triangleq
\mathbf{A}(\theta_{u,\tilde{k}}[t_c^n])\mathbf{p}_{u}[t_c^n]$, $\mathbf{e}_{u,i,l}[t_c^n]
\triangleq
\mathbf{B}(\theta_{u,l}[t_c^n],\theta_{i,l}[t_c^n])
\mathbf{p}_{i}[t_c^n]$.
For given transmit beamforming vectors, UAV trajectories and target association variables, the optimal receive beamforming vector $\mathbf w_{u,k}^{\star}[t^n_c]$ can be obtained by solving the equivalent minimum variance distortionless response
(MVDR) problem for SCNR maximization, which is given by \cite{Lorenz2005MVDR}
\begin{equation}
	\label{receiving beamforming}
	\mathbf w_{u,k}^{\star}[t_c^n]
	=
	\frac{
		\mathbf{\Psi}_{u,k}^{-1}[t_c^n]\mathbf d_{u,k}[t_c^n]
	}{
		\mathbf d^H_{u,k}[t_c^n]\mathbf{\Psi}_{u,k}^{-1}[t_c^n]\mathbf d_{u,k}[t_c^n]
	}.
\end{equation}
By substituting \eqref{receiving beamforming} into \eqref{SCNR}, the SCNR can be re-expressed as
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{u,k}^{\star}[t_c^n]
=
\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]
|\beta_{u,k}[t_c^n]|^2
\mathbf d_{u,k}^H[t_c^n]
\mathbf{\Psi}_{u,k}^{-1}[t_c^n]
\mathbf d_{u,k}[t_c^n].
\end{equation}
Therefore, the radar estimation information rate at the UAV $u$ echoed by target $k$ can be expressed as\cite{SPeng2025}
\begin{align}
R^{\rm sen}_{u,k}[t_c^n]=\frac{\delta}{2\mu}\log_2(1+\gamma_{u,k}^{\star}[t_c^n]),
\end{align}
where $\delta$ and $\mu$ denote the radar duty factor and pulse duration of the radar, respectively.
The corresponding amount of sensing data is
\begin{align}
C^{\rm sen}_{u,k}[t_c^n]=\xi_u[t_c^n]\delta_tR^{\rm sen}_{u,k}[t_c^n].
\end{align}
Then, the total sensed data of UAV $u$ in the slot $t_c^n$ is given by
\begin{equation}
	C_u^{\mathrm{sen}}[t_c^n]=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]C_{u,k}^{\mathrm{sen}}[t_c^n].
\end{equation}
Accordingly, the sensing data processed locally by UAV $u$ at time slot $t_c^n$ is $(1-\lambda_u[t_c^n])C^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]$, and the data required to be offloaded to the USV is $\lambda_u[t_c^n]C^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]$.
\subsection{Communication Model}
Once the UAV finishes sensing task, it will perform local computation and offload part of the sensing data to the USV for further processing simultaneously. It is worth noting that the communication between the UAV and USV is significantly influenced by both the maritime environment and the motion state of the USV. Additionally, since there are few obstructions on the sea surface, the transmission link between the UAV and USV is generally LoS-dominated. Therefore, the channel coefficient vector between the UAV $u$ and the USV can be written as\cite{BLi2025}
\begin{align}
\label{comm_ch}
\mathbf h_u[t_c^n]
=
\sqrt{\rho_u[t_c^n]}
\mathbf b_{\rm eff}
(
\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n]),
\end{align}
where $\rho_u[t_c^n]$ accounts for the large-scale fading attenuation with respect to path loss. $\mathbf b_{\rm eff}
(\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n])
=
\left[
1,
e^{j\pi\sin\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n]},
\cdots,
e^{j\pi(N_{u,t}-1)\sin\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n]}
\right]^T$ is the roll-aware equivalent steering vector with $\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n]$ being the coordinate-rotation-based equivalent elevation angle from the USV to the UAV.
\subsubsection{Model of $\rho_u[t_c^n]$}
In practice, the LoS link and the surface reflection link are two primarily considered paths in an A2S channel. Therefore, the curved-earth two-ray (CE2R) model is adopted to formulate the path loss\cite{Wang2018Wireless, CZeng2021}, which is written by
\begin{equation}
\resizebox{1\hsize}{!}{$\begin{aligned}
	L_{\rm CE2R}[t_c^n]
	=
	-10\log_{10}
	\left(
	\left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d_u[t_c^n]}\right)^2
	\left(
	2\sin\left(
	\frac{2\pi h_t h_r}{\lambda d_u[t_c^n]}
	\right)
	\right)^2
	\right),
\end{aligned}$}
\end{equation}
where $d_u[t_c^n]=\sqrt{(x_u[t_c^n] - x_0[t_c])^2 + (y_u[t_c^n] - y_0[t_c])^2}$ denotes the horizontal distance between UAV $u$ and the USV in time slot $t_c^n$. $\lambda$ is the wavelength. $h_t$ and $h_r$ represent the heights of the transmit and receive antennas, respectively. 
Therefore, the large-scale fading attenuation $\rho_u[t_c^n]$ in \eqref{comm_ch} can be expressed as
\begin{align}
\rho_u[t_c^n]=10^{\frac{-L_{{\rm CE2R}}[t_c^n]} {1 0}}.
\end{align}
\begin{figure}[t]
	\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, trim=40pt 50pt 20pt 160pt, clip]{./USVroll3.pdf}
	\centering
	\caption{\small Coordinate-rotation-based modeling of the roll-aware incident direction for the UAV–USV communication link.}\label{fig3}
	\vspace{-1mm}
\end{figure}
\subsubsection{Model of $\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n]$}
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig3}, we introduce a body-fixed coordinate system to describe the roll motion of the USV\cite{NAhmed2025}, where the $x$-axis points to the bow, the $y$-axis points to the starboard, and the $z$-axis points vertically upward respectively. The roll motion of the USV changes the local coordinate system of the receiving antenna into the coordinate system with axes $x$, $y'$ and $z'$, which further affects the equivalent incident direction. For simplicity,
let $\mathbf q_0^{\rm ant}[t_c]=(x_0[t_c],y_0[t_c],h_r)^T$ denote the USV antenna phase center. The unit link direction vector from the USV to UAV $u$ can be written as
\begin{equation}
\mathbf s_u[t_c^n]
=
\frac{
	\mathbf q_u[t_c^n]
	-
	\mathbf q_0^{\rm ant}[t_c]
}{
	\left\|
	\mathbf q_u[t_c^n]
	-
	\mathbf q_0^{\rm ant}[t_c]
	\right\|
}.
\end{equation}
Then, the roll rotation matrix corresponding to roll angle $\Delta\phi[t_c^n]$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\mathbf R_x(\Delta\phi[t_c^n])
=
\begin{bmatrix}
	1 & 0 & 0
	0 & \cos\Delta\phi[t_c^n] & -\sin\Delta\phi[t_c^n]
	0 & \sin\Delta\phi[t_c^n] & \cos\Delta\phi[t_c^n]
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
To characterize the incident direction observed in the roll-rotated local coordinate system of the USV, the global link direction vector $\mathbf s_u[t_c^n]$ is transformed into the USV local coordinate system as
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\mathbf s}_u[t_c^n]
=
\mathbf R_x^T(\Delta\phi[t_c^n])
\mathbf s_u[t_c^n]=\left(
\widetilde{s}_{x,u}[t_c^n],\;
\widetilde{s}_{y,u}[t_c^n],\;
\widetilde{s}_{z,u}[t_c^n]
\right)^T,
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{s}_{x,u}[t_c^n]$, $\widetilde{s}_{y,u}[t_c^n]$ and $\widetilde{s}_{z,u}[t_c^n]$ denote the $x$-axis, $y$-axis and $z$-axis components of $\widetilde{\mathbf s}_u[t_c^n]$, respectively, in the roll-rotated USV local coordinate system.
Then, the roll-aware equivalent elevation angle is obtained as
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\theta}_u[t_c^n]=
\arcsin
\left(
\widetilde{s}_{z,u}[t_c^n]
\right).
\end{equation}
\subsubsection{Model of $\Delta\phi[t_c^n]$}
Under irregular wave conditions, the USV experiences variations in height and position due to waves. The rolling moment induced by wave disturbances is primarily related to the effective wave inclination angle $\phi_{e} (t)$, which can be modeled as\cite{JYu2024}
\begin{align}
	\phi_e(t) =
	K_1 K_2 \sin \chi \sum_{i=1}^{M} \left[ \sqrt{2S(\omega_i)\Delta\omega_i} \cos(\omega_{i}t + \varepsilon_i) \right],
\end{align}
where $M$ represents the number of waves in a random wave spectrum, and \(S(\omega)\) denotes the spectral density function of wave inclination angles. \(\omega_i\) is the angular frequency of the \(i\)-th wave,
$\Delta\omega_{i}$ is the frequency increment, and $\varepsilon_{i}$ represents the initial phase uniformly distributed within the range $[ 0, 2 \pi) $. $K_1$ and
$K_2$ denote the correction coefficients for finite ship width and
finite draft depth, respectively, $\chi$ is the angle of arrival.
Using the dynamic equations and the relationship between the wave disturbance moments and the effective wave inclination angle, the corresponding Laplace transform can be performed to derive the transfer function between the rolling angle and the effective wave inclination angle as
\begin{equation}
	G \left( s \right) \!=\!\frac{\Delta\phi\left( s \right)} {\phi_{e} \left( s \right)} \!=\! \frac{\omega_{\Delta\phi}^{2} \left( \frac{\Delta J_{\Delta\phi}} {D h} s^{2}+\frac{2 \zeta_{\Delta\phi}} {\omega_{\Delta\phi}} s \!+\! 1 \right)} {s^{2}+2 \zeta_{\Delta\phi} \omega_{\Delta\phi} s+\omega_{\Delta\phi}^{2}} ,
\end{equation}
where the parameters follow the standard ship roll model \cite{JYu2024}.
Therefore, the continuous-time roll response is obtained as
$
\Delta\phi_c(\tau)
= \sum_{i=1}^{M}A_i
\left|G(j\omega_{i})\right|
\cos
\left(
\omega_{i}\tau
+
\varepsilon_i
+
\angle G(j\omega_{i})
\right),
$
where $A_i=
K_1K_2\sin\chi
\sum_{i=1}^{M}
\sqrt{2S(\omega_i)\Delta\omega_i}$.
Then, the roll angle used in the slot $t_c^n$ is obtained by sampling the continuous-time roll response at the slot, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\Delta\phi[t_c^n]
=
\Delta\phi_c([(c-1)N+n-1]\delta_t).
\end{equation}
Let $s_{u,c} [ t_c^n ] \sim {\cal C N} ( 0, 1 ) $ denote the signal transmitted by UAV $u$ to USV. By applying the beamforming vector $\mathbf{f}_u[t_c^n]$, the transmitted communication signal at the UAV $u$ in the time slot $t_c^n$ is given by
\begin{align}
\mathbf{x}_{u,c}[t_c^n]=\mathbf{f}_{u}[t_c^n]s_{u,c}[t_c^n].
\end{align}
Assume that the dedicated sensing signal is prior known by the USV, such that the sensing interference can be cancelled in advance. Therefore, the received signal at the USV in the time slot $t_c^n$ is represented as
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{y}[ t_c^n ]=\sum_{u=1}^{U} {\bf{h}}_{u}^{H} [ t_c^n ] {\bf{f}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] s_{u,c} [ t_c^n ]+n_{u ,c} [ t_c^n ],
\end{equation}
where $n_{u,c} [t_c^n]\sim\mathcal{CN}( 0, \sigma_{u,c}^{2} )$ represents the AWGN. And the achievable rate from UAV $u$ to the USV at time slot $t_c^n$ can be written as
\begin{equation}
 R^{\rm com}_{u} [ t_c^n ]=\log_2 \left( 1+\frac{\left| {\bf{h}}_{u}^{H} [ t_c^n ] {\bf{f}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] \right|^{2}} {\sum_{i=1, i \neq u}^{U} \left| {\bf{h}}_{i}^{H} [ t_c^n ] {\bf{f}}_{i} [ t_c^n ] \right|^{2}+\sigma_{u,c}^{2}} \right).
\end{equation}
Therefore, the maximum amount of the offloading data at UAV $u$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}
C^{\rm com}_u[t_c^n]=B_u(1-\xi_u[t_c^n])\delta_tR^{\rm com}_u[t_c^n].
\end{align}
\subsection{Computation Model}
Each UAV is equipped with a MEC server to process tasks locally. Let $f_{u,\mathrm{loc}}[t_c^n]$ denote the local CPU-cycle frequency (cycle/s) of UAV $u$ in slot $t_c^n$, and let $l_u[t_c^n]$ denote the CPU cycles (in cycles/bit) required for processing one bit of local data. Then, the local computation rate of UAV $u$ can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
	R_{u,\text{loc}}^{\text{comp}}[t_c^n] = \frac{f_{u,\text{loc}}[t_c^n]}{l_{u}[t_c^n]}.
\end{equation}
Accordingly, the maximum amount of data that can be locally processed by UAV $u$ in slot $t_c^n$ is
\begin{equation}
	C_{u,\mathrm{loc}}^{\mathrm{comp}}[t_c^n]=R_{u,\mathrm{loc}}^{\mathrm{comp}}[t_c^n](1-\xi_{u}[t_c^n])\delta_t.
	\end{equation}

For simplicity, we consider the locally processed tasks are required to be completed within the current slot. Therefore, the amount of data assigned to local computing cannot exceed the local computation capability of UAVs:
\begin{equation}
	(1-\lambda_u[t_c^n])C_u^{\mathrm{sen}}[t_c^n]\le C_{u,\mathrm{loc}}^{\mathrm{comp}}[t_c^n], \quad \forall u, c, n.
\end{equation}
In addition, let $l_0 [t_c^n]$ denote the required CPU cycles for processing one bit of data, and $f_{u,0} [t_c^n]$ represent the CPU frequency allocated by the USV to UAV $u$. Then the computation rate at the USV is given by
\begin{equation}
	R^{\rm comp}_{u,0} [t_c^n]=\frac{f_{u,0} [t_c^n]} {l_0 [t_c^n]}.
\end{equation}
Then, the number of computation bits computed by the USV for UAV $u$ at time slot $t_c^n$ can be given as
\begin{equation}
	C_{u,0}^{\rm comp}[t_c^n]=R^{\rm comp}_{u,0} [t_c^n] \delta_t.
\end{equation}
To characterize the dependence of classification performance on
the amount of sensing data, we adopt a sensing-data-based
classification-error surrogate, 
which is given by \cite{Zhang2022Accelerating}
\begin{align}
e_u[t_c^n]=a_u[t_c^n](\max\left\{
N_u^{\rm sam}[t_c^n],\,1
\right\})^{-b_u[t_c^n]},
\end{align}
where $a_u[t_c^n]$ and $b_u[t_c^n]$ are hyperparameters of difficulty of the data from the UAV $u$. $N_u^{\rm sam}[t_c^n]$ is the number of samples that the communication can afford, which can be written as
$
N_u^{\rm sam}[t_c^n]=\frac{C^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]}{D_s},
$
where $D_s$ denotes the data size per sample.
\subsection{Energy Consumption Model}
\subsubsection{USV Energy Consumption Model}
The USV energy consumption at time slot $t_c^n$ is given by
\begin{align}
E_{0} [ t_c^n ]=& \sum_{u=1}^{U} \kappa_0 \delta_t \left( f_{u,0} [ t_c^n ] \right)^{3}, 
\end{align}
where $\kappa_0$ is a constant related to the hardware specifications of the USV.
\subsubsection{UAV Energy Consumption Model}
The UAV energy consumption consists of propulsion, computation, sensing, and offloading energy. Specifically, the propulsion energy of UAV $u$ at time slot $t_c^n$ is given by \cite{Zeng2019UAVEnergy}
\begin{align}
 E^{\rm prop}_{u}[t_c^n]=& \left(P_{\rm bla} \Biggl( 1+\frac{3 \| {\bf{v}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] \|^{2}} {U_{\mathrm{t i p}}^{2}} \Biggr)+\frac{1} {2} d_{0} \rho_{0} s A \| {\bf{v}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] \|^{3}\right.\nonumber  
 &\left.+ \, P_{\rm ind} \! \left( \sqrt{1+\frac{\| {\bf{v}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] \|^{4}} {4 v_{0}^{4}}}-\frac{\| {\bf{v}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] \|^{2}} {2 v_{0}^{2}} \right)^{\frac{1} {2}}\right)\delta_t, 
\end{align}
where the parameters follow the conventional rotary-wing UAV energy model \cite{Zeng2019UAVEnergy}.
The local computation energy consumption of UAV $u$ in the slot $t_c^n$ is modeled as
\begin{equation}
	E_{u}^{\mathrm{loc}}[t_c^n]=\kappa_u (1-\lambda_u[t_c^n])C^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]l_u[t_c^n] \left(f_{u,\mathrm{loc}}[t_c^n]\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $\kappa_u$ is the effective switched capacitance coefficient related to the hardware capability of UAV $u$.
In addition, the sensing and offloading energy consumption  of the UAV $u$ are respectively given by
\begin{equation}
	E^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]=\|\mathbf{p}_u[t_c^n]\|^2\xi_u[t_c^n]\delta_t,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
	E_{u}^{\mathrm{o f f}} [ t_c^n ]=\left\| {\bf{f}}_{u} [ t_c^n ] \right\|^{2} \left( 1-\xi_{u} [ t_c^n ] \right) \delta_{t}.
\end{equation}
Therefore, the total energy consumption of UAV $u$ is
$
	E_{u}[t_c^n]= E^{\rm prop}_u[t_c^n]+E^{\rm loc}_u[t_c^n]+E^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]+E^{\rm off}_u[t_c^n],
$
and the total energy consumption of the system is
$
	E_{sum}[t_c^n]= \sum_{u=1}^{U}E_u[t_c^n]+\eta E_0[t_c^n],
$
where \(\eta\) is a weighting coefficient for the USV computation energy consumption.
\subsection{Dynamic Queue Scheduling Model}
It is worth noting that sensing and computation offloading generate continuous data flows at both UAV and USV sides. At the UAV side, increasing sensing time improves inference performance but produces more sensing data, while allocating more communication time enhances offloading capability, leading to a sensing–communication trade-off. When the generated data exceed the transmission capability, UAV-side data backlog occurs and may degrade network stability. Similarly, at the USV side, the aggregated offloading tasks from multiple UAVs may exceed the computation service capability, resulting in computation backlog. Therefore, UAV-side and USV-side queues are constructed to characterize the end-to-end data flow and maintain system stability.
For notational convenience, we define the next small slot of \(t_c^n\) as
\begin {equation}
\operatorname {next}(t_c^n)=
\begin {cases}
t_c^{n+1}, & n<N,
t_{c+1}^{1}, & n=N.
\end {cases}
\end {equation}
Specifically, in the time slot $t_c^n$, we define the amount of data actually transmitted from UAV $u$ to the USV as \(D_u^{\text{off}}[t_c^n]=\min\left\{Q_u^A[t_c^n]+\lambda_u[t_c^n] C_u^{\text{sen}}[t_c^n],C_u^{\text{com}}[t_c^n]\right\}\), then the data offloading queue at the UAV $u$ can be expressed as $Q_u^A[\operatorname{next}(t_c^n)]=\max\{Q_u^A[t_c^n]-C_u^{\rm com}[t_c^n]+\lambda_u[t_c^n]C_u^{\rm sen}[t_c^n],0\}$. Similarly, the computation queue corresponding to the offloaded tasks from UAV $u$ at the USV is updated as
	$Q_u^V[\operatorname{next}(t_c^n)]
	=
	\max\{Q_u^V[t_c^n]-C_{u,0}^{\rm comp}[t_c^n],0\}
	+
	D_u^{\text{off}}[t_c^n]$.
To ensure the stability of the data queue, the queue length of all devices should not increase infinitely, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
	\lim_{C\to+\infty}
	\frac{1}{CN}
	\sum_{c=1}^{C}
	\sum_{n=1}^{N}
	\mathbb E(Q_u^A[t_c^n])
	< \infty,\quad \forall u.
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
	\lim_{C\to+\infty}
	\frac{1}{CN}
	\sum_{c=1}^{C}
	\sum_{n=1}^{N}
	\mathbb E(Q_u^V[t_c^n])
	< \infty,\quad \forall u.
\end{equation}
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
We aim to minimize long-term time-averaged total energy consumption subject to the system stability and performance requirement. Under the two-timescale system model, let $\Omega=\{\{\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]\},\{\mathbf{q}_u[t_c]\},\{\mathbf{p}_u[t_c^n]\},\{\mathbf{f}_u[t_c^n]\},\{\xi_u[t_c^n]\},\{\lambda_u[t_c^n]\},\{f_{u,0}[t_c^n]\}\}$, then the optimization problem can be formulated as
\begin{subequations}
	\begin{align}
		\text{(P1):}\quad &
		\min_{\Omega}\quad
		\lim_{C\to+\infty}
		\frac{1}{CN}
		\sum_{c=1}^{C}
		\sum_{n=1}^{N}
		\mathbb E\left(E_{\rm sum}[t_c^n]\right)
		\mathrm{s.t.}\quad
		&\xi_{u}[t_c^n]R_{u,k}^{\rm sen}[t_c^n]
		\ge
		\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]R_{\rm th}^{\rm sen},
		\quad \forall u,k,c,n, \label{eq:sensing}
		&\|\mathbf p_u[t_c^n]\|^2\le P_u^{\max},
		\quad \forall u,c,n, \label{eq:sensing beamfoming}
		&\|\mathbf f_u[t_c^n]\|^2\le P_u^{\max},
		\quad \forall u,c,n,\label{eq:communication beamforming}
		&0\le \xi_u[t_c^n]\le 1,\quad \forall u,c,n,\label{eq:xi}
		&0\le \lambda_u[t_c^n]\le 1,\quad \forall u,c,n,\label{eq:lambda}
		&\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]\in\{0,1\},
		\quad \forall u,k,c,\label{eq:alpha}
		&\sum_{u=1}^{U}\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]\le 1, 
		\quad \forall k,c,\label{eq:total UAV alpha}
		&\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]\le K_u^{\max},
		\quad \forall u,c,\label{eq:total target alpha}
		&(1-\lambda_u[t_c^n])C_u^{\rm sen}[t_c^n]
		\le
		C_{u,\rm loc}^{\rm comp}[t_c^n],
		\quad \forall u,c,n,\label{eq:local}
		&f_{u,0}[t_c^n]\ge 0,\quad
		\sum_{u=1}^{U}f_{u,0}[t_c^n]\le f_0^{\max},
		\quad \forall c,n,\label{eq:computation}
		&\mathbf q_u[t_1]=\mathbf q_{u,I},
		\quad \forall u,\label{eq:start}
		&\|\mathbf q_u[t_c^n]-\mathbf q_{u'}[t_c^n]\|\ge d_{\min},
		\quad u\ne u',\forall c,n,\label{eq:security}
		&
		\|\mathbf v_u[t_c^n]\|
		\le
		v_{\max},
		\quad \forall u,c,n, \label{eq:speed}
		&e_u[t_c^n]\le e_{\rm th},
		\quad \forall u,c,n, \label{eq:train}
		&\lim_{C\to+\infty}
		\frac{1}{CN}
		\sum_{c=1}^{C}
		\sum_{n=1}^{N}
		\mathbb E(Q_u^A[t_c^n])
		< \infty,\quad \forall u, \label{eq:UAV queue}
		&\lim_{C\to+\infty}
		\frac{1}{CN}
		\sum_{c=1}^{C}
		\sum_{n=1}^{N}
		\mathbb E(Q_u^V[t_c^n])
		< \infty,\quad \forall u. \label{eq:USV queue}
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where \eqref{eq:sensing} guarantees the sensing performance requirement, while \eqref{eq:sensing beamfoming} and \eqref{eq:communication beamforming} constrain the sensing and communication power, respectively. \eqref{eq:xi} and \eqref{eq:lambda} define the feasible ranges of time allocation and offloading ratio. \eqref{eq:alpha}–\eqref{eq:total target alpha} represent UAV-target association constraints. \eqref{eq:local} and \eqref{eq:computation} restrict the UAV and USV computation resources, respectively. \eqref{eq:start}–\eqref{eq:speed} ensure UAV mobility constraints, while \eqref{eq:train} and \eqref{eq:UAV queue}–\eqref{eq:USV queue} guarantee inference accuracy and queue stability.
\section{Problem Transformation}
\label{sec:problem}
Due to the time-averaged objective function and long-term constraints \eqref{eq:UAV queue} and \eqref{eq:USV queue}, it is difficult to solve the problem (P1) directly. Therefore, we introduce the Lyapunov optimization method to transform the problem. 
The Lyapunov optimization is employed to address the long-term stochastic problem. The collections of UAV data queues $Q^A_u[t_c^n]$ and USV data queues $Q^V_u[t_c^n]$ can be denoted as $Q^A[t_c^n] \triangleq \left[ Q_1^A[t_c^n], Q_2^A[t_c^n], \dots, Q_U^A[t_c^n] \right]$ and $Q^V[t_c^n] \triangleq \left[ Q_1^V[t_c^n], Q_2^V[t_c^n], \dots, Q_U^V[t_c^n] \right]$.
Let $\Theta(t_c^n) \triangleq \left\{ Q^{A}[t_c^n], Q^{V}[t_c^n] \right\}$, and then the Lyapunov function is 
\begin{equation}
	L ( \Theta( t_c^n ) )=\frac{1} {2} \sum_{u=1}^{U} [ Q^A_{u} [ t_c^n ] ]^{2}+\frac{1} {2} \sum_{u=1}^{U} [ Q^V_{u} [ t_c^n ] ]^{2}.
\end{equation}
It can be found that the smaller the value of $L ( \Theta( t_c^n ) )$ is, the less queue backlog in the queue is. Without loss of generality, we assume that $L ( \Theta( 0 ) )=0$. 
We define the Lyapunov drift as the expected change in the
Lyapunov function over one time slot \cite{Bi2021Lyapunov}, that is
\begin{equation}
	\Delta( \Theta( t_c^n ) )=E [L ( \Theta( \operatorname{next}(t_c^n) ) )-L ( \Theta( t_c^n ) ) | \Theta( t_c^n ) ].
\end{equation}
To ensure the stability of the system, we should minimize the Lyapunov drift at each time slot and maintain the queue backlog at a low level. Obviously, reducing the queue backlog generally requires higher transmission and computation rates, which inevitably increase energy consumption. Therefore, to minimize the total energy consumption while maintaining queue stability, we first define the drift-plus-penalty function to establish a connection between the Lyapunov function and energy consumption, which can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
	\Delta( \Theta( t_c^n ) ) +\! V \! \mathbb{E} [E_ {\rm sum}[t_c^n]| \Theta( t_c^n )],
\end{equation}
where $V$ denotes the trade-off between the total energy consumption and Lyapunov drift, reflecting whether the current system places more emphasis on energy minimization or system stability. Specifically, the larger the value of $V$, the closer the total energy consumption of the system is to the optimal value. Conversely, a lower $V$ value focuses on minimizing the Lyapunov drift.
Directly solving the optimization problem of the given expression incurs high computational complexity. Therefore, we further introduce an upper bound of the drift-plus-penalty, as shown in the following Theorem 1.
\begin{theorem}
	For $\forall \Theta(t_c^n), \forall t \in \mathcal{T}$, the drift-plus-penalty has the following upper bound
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{aligned} 
 {{}} & {{} {{} \Delta( \Theta( t_c^n ) )+V \mathbb{E} [ E_{\rm s u m} [ t_c^n ] | \Theta( t_c^n ) ] \leq R+V \mathbb{E} [ E_{\rm s u m} [ t_c^n ] | \Theta( t_c^n ) ]}}  {{}} & {{} {{}+\mathbb{E} \Big\{\sum_{u=1}^{U} Q^A_{u} [ t_c^n ] \Big( \lambda_u[t_c^n]C^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]-C_u^{\mathrm{com}}[t_c^n] \Big) | \Theta( t_c^n ) \Big\}}}  {{}} & {{} {{}+\mathbb{E} \Big\{\sum_{u=1}^{U} Q^V_{u} [ t_c^n ] \Big( C_u^{\mathrm{com}}[t_c^n]-C_{u,0}^{\rm comp}[t_c^n] \Big) | \Theta( t_c^n ) \Big\},}} 
		\end{aligned} 
	\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
where $R$ is a bounded constant. 
\textit{Proof:} A similar proof has been provided in our previous work \cite{YWang2026-1} and is omitted here for brevity.
Finally, according to the Lyapunov optimization method, the stochastic optimization P1 is transformed to focus on minimizing the Lyapunov drift-plus-penalty upper bound in each time slot, by disregarding the fixed term $R$, P1 can be formulated as follows
\begin{align}
	\text{(P2):}\quad & \min_{\Omega} 
	\mathcal{G}[t_c^n] 
	\text{s.t.} \quad & \eqref{eq:sensing}-\eqref{eq:train}, \nonumber
\end{align}
where  the specific form of the objective function is
\begin{align}
		\mathcal{G}[t_c^n]=&V  E_{\rm s u m} [ t_c^n ]+  \sum_{u=1}^{U} Q^A_{u} [ t_c^n ] \Big( \lambda_u[t_c^n]C^{\rm sen}_u[t_c^n]-C_u^{\mathrm{com}}[t_c^n] \Big)\nonumber&+\sum_{u=1}^{U} Q^V_{u} [ t_c^n ] \Big( C_u^{\mathrm{com}}[t_c^n]-C_{u,0}^{\rm comp}[t_c^n] \Big).
\end{align}
\section{Lyapunov-Guided Dual-Timescale Hierarchical Soft Actor-Critic Algorithm}
\label{sec:algorithm}
For problem (P2), the resulting mixed-integer dual-timescale nonlinear optimization problem is highly coupled, where large-timescale decisions, including UAV association and trajectory planning, affect subsequent small-timescale resource allocation, while slot-level variables are jointly coupled through sensing, communication, computation, and queue evolution. 
To address this challenge, we propose an LTH-SAC framework, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig4}. At the beginning of each frame \(t_c\), the high-level policy observes the frame-level state and outputs the frame-level action including UAV-target association, UAV position, and reference values of time allocation and offloading ratio. Since the high-level action contains discrete and continuous variables, SAC with Gumbel-Softmax is adopted to handle the hybrid action space. Then, given the high-level action within each frame, the low-level policy adjusts slot-level resource allocation, including time allocation, offloading ratio, beamforming, and USV computing resources. After the low-level policy executes the
slot-level actions for all \(N\) small slots in frame \(t_c\), the high-level policy receives an aggregated frame-level reward based on slot-level outcomes and updates the next frame state.
