\subsection{Large-Timescale Decision Based on Hybrid SAC}
To capture the long-term impact of UAV-target association, UAV trajectory,
and service-mode reference decisions under the dynamic maritime environment,
the high-level policy adopts a hybrid SAC framework with Gumbel-Softmax
reparameterization, whose state space, action space, and reward are defined as follows.
\subsubsection{State Space}For the high-level policy, 
the large-timescale state space in frame \(t_c\) can be defined as
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		s_H[t_c]=
		\Big\{
		&Q^A[t_c^1],
 Q^V[t_c^1], \mathbf q_0[t_c], \Delta\phi[t_c^1],
		&\{\mathbf q_u[t_c], \mathbf{g}_k[t_c],  \rho_u[t_c^1], \bar{e}_u[t_{c-1}] \}_{u\in\mathcal U, k\in\mathcal K} \Big\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{e}_u[t_{c-1}]$ is the statistical information classification-error state.
\subsubsection{Action Space}Corresponding to problem (P2), the high-level action space 
can be described by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		a_H[t_c]=
		\Big\{
		&\{\alpha_{u,k}[t_c],
		\mathbf q_u[t_{c+1}],
		\bar{\xi}_u[t_c],
 \bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]\}_{u\in\mathcal U,k\in\mathcal K}
		\Big\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{\xi}_u[t_c]$ and $\bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]$ are reference values of the time allocation factor and offloading ratio, respectively.
Since the high-level action contains both discrete and continuous variables,
the high-level policy is factorized as
\begin{equation}
	\pi_H(a_H[t_c]|s_H[t_c])
	=
	\pi_H^{d}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}[t_c]|s_H[t_c])
	\pi_H^{c}(\mathbf a_H^{c}[t_c]|s_H[t_c]),
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\alpha}[t_c]=\{\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]\}_{u\in\mathcal U,k\in\mathcal K}$ denotes the discrete
association action, and
	$\mathbf a_H^{c}[t_c]
	=
	\left\{
	\{\mathbf q_u[t_{c+1}]\},
	\{\bar{\xi}_u[t_c]\},
	\{\bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]\}
	\right\}$
denotes the continuous high-level action.
For the discrete UAV-target association, the high-level actor outputs logits
\(\ell_{k,j}[t_c]\) for assigning target \(k\) to option
\(j\in\{0,\cdots,U\}\), where \(j=0\) means that target \(k\) is not assigned
to any UAV. The Gumbel-Softmax reparameterization is used to generate a
differentiable approximation of the discrete association decision:
\begin{equation}
	\tilde y_{k,j}[t_c]
	=
	\frac{
		\exp\left((\ell_{k,j}[t_c]+g_{k,j})/\tau_g\right)
	}{
		\sum_{j'=0}^{U}
		\exp\left((\ell_{k,j'}[t_c]+g_{k,j'})/\tau_g\right)
	},
\end{equation}
where \(g_{k,j}=-\log(-\log \epsilon_{k,j})\),
\(\epsilon_{k,j}\sim \mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)\), and \(\tau_g\) is the Gumbel
temperature. During training, \(\tilde y_{k,j}[t_c]\) is used for
differentiable backpropagation.
During execution, the discrete association is obtained by selecting the maximum probability output and then corrected to satisfy association constraints.










The continuous high-level action is generated by the reparameterization trick
in SAC, and the raw continuous output is projected into the feasible region to satisfy constraints.
\subsubsection{Reward}We define the high-level reward for executing the action $a_H[t_c]$ based
on the state $s_H[t_c]$ during frame \(t_c\) as
\begin{equation}
	r_H[t_c]
	=
	\sum_{n=1}^{N} r_L[t_c^n]
	-
	\mathcal P_H[t_c],
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal P_H[t_c]\) denotes the penalty for violating large-timescale
constraints, including the UAV-target association constraints, UAV speed
constraints, and collision avoidance constraints.
\subsubsection{Neural Networks}In the high-level SAC, two soft Q-functions \(Q_{H,1}\) and \(Q_{H,2}\) are
used to reduce the overestimation bias. The high-level transition is stored as $\left(s_H[t_c],a_H[t_c],r_H[t_c],s_H[t_{c+1}]\right)$ in the high-level replay buffer \(\mathcal D_H\). For a mini-batch sampled
from \(\mathcal D_H\), the high-level soft target value is given by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		y_H
		=
		r_H[t_c]
		+
		\gamma_H
		\Big[
		&\min_{i=1,2}
		Q_{H,i}^{-}(s_H[t_{c+1}],a_H'[t_{c+1}])
		&-
		\eta_H
		\log\pi_{H,\theta}(a_H'[t_{c+1}]|s_H[t_{c+1}])
		\Big],
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(a_H'[t_{c+1}]\sim\pi_H(\cdot|s_H[t_{c+1}])\),
\(Q_{H,i}^{-}\) is the target Q-network with parameter $\psi_{H,i}^{-}$, \(\gamma_H\) is the high-level
discount factor, and \(\eta_H\) is the entropy temperature. The high-level Q-networks are updated by minimizing the temporal-difference error between the estimated Q-values and the soft target values, which is given by
\begin{equation}
	J_Q^H(\psi_{H,i})
	=
	\mathbb E_{\mathcal D_H}
	\left[
	\left(
	Q_{H,i}(s_H[t_c],a_H[t_c];\psi_{H,i})-y_H
	\right)^2
	\right],
\end{equation}
where \(i\in\{1,2\}\), and \(\psi_{H,i}\) denotes the parameter of the
\(i\)-th high-level Q-network.
The high-level policy network is updated by minimizing the entropy-regularized
policy objective, which is given by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		J_{\pi}^H(\theta_H)
		=
		\mathbb E_{s_H[t_c]\sim\mathcal D_H}
		\Big[
		&\eta_H
		\log\pi_H(a_H[t_c]|s_H[t_c])
		&-
		\min_{i=1,2}Q_{H,i}(s_H[t_c],a_H[t_c])
		\Big],
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(\theta_H\) denotes the parameter of the high-level policy network. The target Q-networks are then updated by the following rule 
\begin{equation}
	\psi_{H,i}^- \leftarrow
	\tau_H\psi_{H,i}+(1-\tau_H)\psi_{H,i}^-,
	\quad i\in\{1,2\},
\end{equation}
where \(\tau_H\in[0,1]\) is the soft update coefficient.
\subsection{Small-Timescale Resource Allocation Based on MASAC}
Due to the continuous nature and high-dimensional coupling characteristics of slot-level decision variables, we adopt a centralized training with decentralized execution framework and design a cooperative scheme based on MASAC. During the training phase, all UAV agents are jointly optimized by leveraging global state information. In the execution phase, each agent independently outputs its actions only according to its local observation.
Specifically, the set of low-level agents is defined as
$\mathcal I_L=\{0,1,\cdots,U\}$,
where \(u\in\{1,\cdots,U\}\) denotes UAV agent $u$, and \(0\)
denotes the USV agent. 
\subsubsection{Environment State Space}The global state space of the low-level multi-agent SAC in
slot \(t_c^n\) includes queues, UAV-USV channel and the USV roll angle information, which is given by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		S_L[t_c^n]=
		\Big\{
		&a_H[t_c],
		Q^A[t_c^n],
 Q^V[t_c^n], \{\mathbf h_u[t_c^n]\}_{u\in\mathcal U},
		\Delta\phi[t_c^n],
		&\{\mathbf q_u[t_c^n], \rho_u[t_c^n],\theta_{u,k}[t_c^n]\}_{u\in\mathcal U,k\in\mathcal K}
		\Big\},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(a_H[t_c]\) is the high-level action.
\subsubsection{Observation Space}In the partially observable ISCC
environment, local observations for UAV agent $u$ and the USV
agent in the slot $t^n_c$ are detailed below.
For the UAV agent \(u\), it observes the states of itself and targets, as well as the roll angle state of the USV, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		o_{u,L}[t_c^n]=
		\Big\{
		&\{\alpha_{u,k}[t_c]\}_{k\in\mathcal K},
		\mathbf q_u[t_c^n], 
		Q_u^A[t_c^n],
		Q_u^V[t_c^n],\mathbf h_u[t_c^n],
		&
		\Delta\phi[t_c^n],
		\{\theta_{u,k}[t_c^n], d_{u,k}[t_c^n]]\}_{k\in\mathcal K},
		\bar{\xi}_u[t_c],
		\bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]
		\Big\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
For the USV agent, it observes the states of itself and UAVs, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		o_{0,L}[t_c^n]
		=
		\Big\{
		&a_H[t_c],
 Q^A[t_c^n],
 Q^V[t_c^n],
		\{\mathbf h_u[t_c^n]\}_{u\in\mathcal U},
		\Delta\phi[t_c^n]
		\Big\}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\subsubsection{Action Space}
Based on the associated observational data $\{o_{u,L}[t_c^n], o_{0,L}[t_c^n]\}$, each UAV and the USV make decisions of action. In the time slot $t_c^n$, the UAV agent \(u\) outputs the action
\begin{equation}
	a_{u,L}[t_c^n]
	=
	\left\{
	\Delta\xi_u[t_c^n],
	\Delta\lambda_u[t_c^n],
	\mathbf p_u[t_c^n],
	\mathbf f_u[t_c^n]
	\right\},
\end{equation}
and the USV agent outputs the computing resource allocation action
\begin{equation}
	a_{0,L}[t_c^n]=
	\{f_{u,0}[t_c^n]\}_{u\in\mathcal U}.
\end{equation}





Accordingly, the joint low-level action is denoted by
\begin{equation}
	A_L[t_c^n]
	=
	\left\{
	a_{0,L}[t_c^n],
	a_{1,L}[t_c^n],
	\cdots,
	a_{U,L}[t_c^n]
	\right\}.
\end{equation}
Given the high-level reference values \(\bar{\xi}_u[t_c]\) and
\(\bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]\), the actual slot-level time allocation factor and
offloading ratio are reconstructed as $\xi_u[t_c^n]
	=
	\Pi_{[0,1]}
	\left(
	\bar{\xi}_u[t_c]+\Delta\xi_u[t_c^n]
	\right),$
$
	\lambda_u[t_c^n]
	=
	\Pi_{[\lambda_u^{\min}[t_c^n],1]}
	\left(
	\bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]+\Delta\lambda_u[t_c^n]
	\right).
$
















\subsubsection{Reward}According to the Lyapunov-guided objective in (P2), we define a
shared cooperative reward for all low-level agents as
\begin{equation}
	r_L[t_c^n]
	=
	-\mathcal{G}[t_c^n]
	-
	\mathcal P_L[t_c^n],
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal P_L[t_c^n]\) denotes the penalty for violating
small-timescale constraints, including the transmit power constraints, local
computing constraints, sensing-rate constraints, classification-error
constraints, and USV computing resource constraints. By using a shared reward,
all low-level agents are encouraged to cooperatively reduce the system energy
consumption while maintaining queue stability.
\subsubsection{Neural Networks}Each low-level agent \(i\in\mathcal I_L\) maintains a stochastic
actor network
\(\pi_{\theta_{L,i}}(a_{i,L}[t_c^n]|o_{i,L}[t_c^n])\), parameterized by
\(\theta_{L,i}\), for generating slot-level actions from local observations.
Meanwhile, two centralized soft Q-networks
\(Q_{L,i,1}(S_L[t_c^n],A_L[t_c^n];\psi_{L,i,1})\) and
\(Q_{L,i,2}(S_L[t_c^n],A_L[t_c^n];\psi_{L,i,2})\) are employed to evaluate the
global state-action value and mitigate overestimation bias. During training,
all agents collect their local experience tuple
to form the global transition
$\left\langle
	S_L[t_c^n],
	A_L[t_c^n],
	r_L[t_c^n],
	S_L[\mathrm{next}(t_c^n)]
	\right\rangle,$
which is stored in the low-level replay buffer \(\mathcal D_L\).
For a mini-batch sampled from \(\mathcal D_L\), the target value of the
\(i\)-th low-level agent is given by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		y_{L,i}
		=
		r_L[t_c^n]
		+
		\gamma_L
		\Big[
		&\min_{\ell=1,2}
		Q_{L,i,\ell}^{-}
		\left(S_L',A_L';\psi_{L,i,\ell}^{-}\right)
		&-
		\eta_L
		\sum_{j\in\mathcal I_L}
		\log
		\pi_{\theta_{L,j}}
		\left(a_{j,L}'|o_{j,L}'\right)
		\Big],
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(S_L'=S_L[\mathrm{next}(t_c^n)]\),
\(A_L'=\{a_{j,L}'\}_{j\in\mathcal I_L}\),
\(a_{j,L}'\sim\pi_{\theta_{L,j}}(\cdot|o_{j,L}')\),
\(Q_{L,i,\ell}^{-}\) is the target Q-network, \(\gamma_L\) is the low-level
discount factor, and \(\eta_L\) is the entropy temperature.
Then, the low-level Q-networks are updated by minimizing the
temporal-difference error between the estimated Q-value and the target value,
which is given by
\begin{equation}
	\resizebox{1\hsize}{!}{$\begin{aligned}
		J_Q^{L,i}(\psi_{L,i,\ell})
		=
		\mathbb E_{\mathcal D_L}
		\left[
		\left(
		Q_{L,i,\ell}(S_L[t_c^n],A_L[t_c^n];\psi_{L,i,\ell})
		-
		y_{L,i}
		\right)^2
		\right],
		\end{aligned}$}
\end{equation}
where $\ell\in\{1,2\}$.
The low-level policy network of agent \(i\) is updated by minimizing the
entropy-regularized policy objective, which is given by
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}
		J_{\pi}^{L,i}(\theta_{L,i})
		=
		\mathbb E_{\mathcal D_L}
		\Big[
		&\eta_L
		\log
		\pi_{\theta_{L,i}}
		\left(a_{i,L}[t_c^n]|o_{i,L}[t_c^n]\right)
		&-
		\min_{\ell=1,2}
		Q_{L,i,\ell}
		\left(S_L[t_c^n],A_L[t_c^n]\right)
		\Big].
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Finally, the target Q-networks are updated by the soft update rule as follows
\begin{equation}
	\psi_{L,i,\ell}^{-}
	\leftarrow
	\tau_L\psi_{L,i,\ell}
	+
	(1-\tau_L)\psi_{L,i,\ell}^{-},
	\quad i\in\mathcal I_L,\ \ell\in\{1,2\},
\end{equation}
where \(\tau_L\in[0,1]\) is the soft update coefficient.
Based on the proposed LTH-SAC framework, the interaction between
the large-timescale and small-timescale policies is established through
hierarchical decision-making and information exchange, as shown in
Algorithm~\ref{alg:LTHSAC}. At each frame, the high-level hybrid SAC observes the
frame-level state and generates feasible association, waypoint, and
reference decisions. Guided by these decisions, the low-level MASAC
agents perform slot-level resource allocation based on local observations.
The environment then returns rewards and updates system states.
Slot-level transitions are stored in the low-level replay buffer for
MASAC training, while the accumulated frame-level reward is used to
update the high-level SAC after completing all slots within a frame.
\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Proposed LTH-SAC training framework}
	\label{alg:LTHSAC}
	\small
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\State Initialize high-level SAC networks and low-level SAC networks;
		\State Initialize target networks and replay buffers $\mathcal{D}_H$ and $\mathcal{D}_L$;

		\For{episode $=1,2,\cdots$}
		\State Initialize UAV positions, queue states, channel states, target \Statex \hspace{\algorithmicindent}states, and USV roll states;

		\For{frame $c=1,2,\cdots,C$}
		\State Observe high-level state $s_H[t_c]$;
		\State Select high-level action $a_H[t_c]$ according to
		\Statex \hspace{3em}$\pi_H(\cdot|s_H[t_c])$;
		\State Evaluate high-level penalty $P_H[t_c]$;
		\State Correct $a_H[t_c]$ to obtain feasible UAV-target association, \Statex \hspace{3em}next UAV waypoint, and reference values $\bar{\xi}_u[t_c]$, $\bar{\lambda}_u[t_c]$;
		\State Set accumulated high-level reward $r_H[t_c]\leftarrow 0$;

		\For{slot $n=1,2,\cdots,N$}
		\State Observe low-level state $S_L[t_c^n]$ under the guidance of \Statex \hspace{4.5em}$a_H[t_c]$;
		\State For each low-level agent $i\in\mathcal I_L$, select 
		$a_{i,L}[t_c^n]$ \Statex \hspace{4.5em}according to 
		$\pi_{\theta_{L,i}}(\cdot|o_{i,L}[t_c^n])$;
		\State Reconstruct feasible executable variables $\xi_u[t_c^n]$, \Statex \hspace{4.5em}$\lambda_u[t_c^n]$, $\mathbf{p}_u[t_c^n]$, $\mathbf{f}_u[t_c^n]$, and $f_{u,0}[t_c^n]$;
		\State Execute the action and evaluate system performance;
		\State Calculate low-level reward $r_L[t_c^n]$ and update queue \Statex \hspace{4.5em}states;
		\State Store low-level transition in $\mathcal{D}_L$;
		\State Update low-level SAC networks;
		\State Accumulate reward $r_H[t_c]\leftarrow r_H[t_c]+r_L[t_c^n]$;
		\EndFor

		\State $r_H[t_c]\leftarrow r_H[t_c]-P_H[t_c]$;
		\State Obtain next high-level state $s_H[t_{c+1}]$;
		\State Store high-level transition in $\mathcal{D}_H$;
		\State Update high-level SAC networks;
		\EndFor
		\EndFor
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\section{Simulation Results}
\label{sec:results}
We consider a maritime ISCC system consisting of one USV,
$U=6$ UAVs, and $K=10$ stationary maritime targets within a
$1,000~\mathrm{m}\times1,000~\mathrm{m}$ area. UAVs fly at a fixed altitude with a maximum speed of
$v_{\rm max}=20~\mathrm{m/s}$ and a minimum safety distance of
$d_{\rm min}=25~\mathrm{m}$. 
Each UAV is equipped with four transmit antennas and four receive antennas.
Each episode contains 40 large-timescale frames, each consisting of five
time slots with a duration of $1~\mathrm{s}$.
The carrier frequency $f_c$ and communication bandwidth $B_u$ are set to
$5.8~\mathrm{GHz}$ and $1~\mathrm{MHz}$, respectively.
Both the maximum sensing power and communication power of each UAV are
$2~\mathrm{W}$.
The UAV and USV computing capabilities are set to $1~\mathrm{GHz}$ and $5~\mathrm{GHz}$, respectively.
To characterize the maritime environment, the USV roll motion is
generated using 32 irregular-wave components with a significant wave
height of $0.7~\mathrm{m}$.
The remaining key simulation parameters are summarized in
Table~\ref{tab:simulation_parameters} \cite{Zhang2019UAVMEC}.
For the proposed LTH-SAC algorithm, both the high-level and low-level
Actor and Critic adopt two-layer fully connected networks, with each
hidden layer containing 512 neurons.
The high-level and low-level discount factors are set to 0.98 and 0.95,
respectively.
The replay buffer capacity and batch size are
$3\times10^{5}$ and 256, respectively.
The proposed LTH-SAC algorithm is compared with SAC, deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG), and the greedy
algorithm \cite{Xu2022MultiUAV}.
