Fig.~\ref{fig:reward_convergence} evaluates the reward convergence of the algorithms. It can be observed that LTH-SAC gradually improves average reward during early exploration and converges after about 100 episodes to a higher average reward than the benchmarks, with a relatively narrow variance band. This indicates stable training and can be attributed to the hierarchical two-timescale architecture, which effectively coordinates long-timescale decisions with short-timescale resource allocation. Overall, LTH-SAC effectively handles the coupled dual-timescale decisions in the considered multi-UAV maritime ISCC system.
Fig.~\ref{fig:queue_stability} illustrates the queue stability index during training, where a smaller value indicates better queue stability. Although all learning-based algorithms fluctuate during early exploration due to policy exploration and dynamic resource adjustment, the queue stability index of LTH-SAC decreases rapidly and remains at a relatively lower level after convergence than the benchmarks. Fig.~\ref{fig:queue_backlog} presents the queue backlog evolution of UAVs and the USV under the proposed LTH-SAC algorithm. We can observe that all the queue backlogs gradually converge to bounded values after approximately 100 episodes without persistent accumulation. These results demonstrate that LTH-SAC effectively coordinates sensing, communication offloading, and computation processing to maintain favorable system queue stability.
