Fig.~\ref{fig:xi} investigates the impact of the average time allocation ratio \(\xi_u\) on sensing data generation, inference accuracy, and UAV queue backlog. As \(\xi_u\) increases, the amount of sensing data increases and the normalized inference error decreases, while the normalized UAV queue backlog grows. This is because allocating more time to sensing generates more data while reducing the time available for communication, thereby increasing queueing pressure. These results highlight the inherent trade-off between sensing performance and queue stability under limited sensing and communication resources.
Fig.~\ref{fig:lambda_energy_tradeoff} and Fig.~\ref{fig:lambda_queue_tradeoff} further examine the impact of the computation offloading ratio \(\lambda_u\) on energy consumption and queue backlogs. As \(\lambda_u\) increases, more sensed data are offloaded to the USV, reducing UAV local-computing energy while increasing USV computing energy. The total system energy does not vary monotonically with \(\lambda_u\), indicating that excessive offloading does not necessarily minimize the overall energy consumption due to the redistribution of computation and communication loads. Moreover, both the UAV-side and USV-side queue backlogs increase with \(\lambda_u\), showing that aggressive offloading may transfer computation burden to the communication and the USV processing. Therefore, an intermediate offloading level is generally required to balance UAV energy savings, USV computing cost, and queue stability.
Fig.~\ref{fig:V} illustrates the impact of the Lyapunov control parameter \(V\) on the average system energy consumption of different algorithms. As \(V\) increases, the energy consumption of LTH-SAC, SAC, and DDPG decreases, whereas that of the greedy algorithm remains almost unchanged. This trend is consistent with the drift-plus-penalty principle, since a larger $V$ assigns greater importance to energy minimization in the resource-allocation objective. Moreover, LTH-SAC consistently achieves the lowest energy consumption over the entire range of $V$, demonstrating that the proposed hierarchical policy can more effectively coordinate target association, UAV trajectory, time allocation, computation offloading, and communication resources. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:scalability} evaluates the scalability of different algorithms under varying network scales. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:energy_targets}, the total energy consumption generally increases with the number of sensing targets because the UAVs must undertake heavier sensing, communication, and computation workloads. Similarly, Fig.~\ref{fig:energy_uavs} shows that increasing the number of UAVs results in higher propulsion, sensing, computation, and transmission energy consumption. Nevertheless, LTH-SAC consistently achieves the lowest energy consumption in both scenarios. These results demonstrate that LTH-SAC can effectively coordinate UAV trajectories and resource allocation while maintaining superior energy efficiency under different sensing workloads and network scales, thereby validating its adaptability and scalability.
