\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper investigated the joint UAV trajectory planning and resource allocation in a maritime ISCC system, considering maritime environmental effects and coupled UAV–USV queue dynamics. We formulated a dual-timescale stochastic optimization problem and proposed an LTH-SAC framework to minimize long-term energy consumption while maintaining system performance and queue stability. Simulation results demonstrated that LTH-SAC achieves lower energy consumption and better queue stability than benchmark schemes under different network scales. Moreover, allocating more time to sensing improves sensing performance but reduces communication opportunities and increases UAV queue backlogs, while greater computation offloading reduces UAV local computing energy at the expense of increased USV processing load and queueing pressure. These findings highlight the effectiveness of queue-aware resource management in balancing sensing, communication, computation, and system stability in maritime ISCC systems.
